in turn.  \n\n- The equation ![](images/61/61e7a29736499942ecd74ac3f1cde7f58b5c1c88624cccf698f8d5dff0151b0e.png) defines a non-empty set. It represents the point   ![](images/63/63658ff56cb42b62a8aff0dd6883e53dc63e0c7d115acf571e96ed9f63421029.png) in the ![](images/38/38801c8aee855aeef6e7490e7ce6ba196b64443ab6d7032313628f818afb4a79.png)-plane.\n\n- The equation ![](images/95/951eb118d8f44d67994f51091397fc57d8e330053ef011d0cc23bcac40f5cea2.png) defines an empty set. Notice that the left-hand side is non-negative for all ![](images/61/612b633b99184f47ee064ada5f6d901b484b0fe6853cb3a8cb4d90417b5ae99d.png) and ![](images/1e/1e5a2da0565685a7cc228dfa89095c1ef454bbb8b62e9806759223af8b85deec.png) while the right-hand side is negative.\n\n- The equation ![](images/54/546424b4f70a30ccbef3b6e8add2050617bd83978407c00cf88afa63e8f22b5f.png) defines a non-empty set. It represents a  pair of intersecting lines, ![](images/a2/a25e48e9d48f10c78491a71883fbc73e8637ccecafa9f194d0943c77fac46da0.png) and ![](images/df/df4ee978ef8c5458e021927c04566092af64c7f60f3aa1f4c2534e19c2f173be.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I  and III only.\"" 13194139536011 17592186045417 false] [17592186172028 1038 #edb/ref 17592186557222 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186172028 1038 #edb/ref 17592186557253 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186172029 1038 #edb/ref 17592186557250 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186172029 1038 #edb/ref 17592186557300 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186172030 1038 #edb/ref 17592186557324 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186172030 1038 #edb/ref 17592186557330 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186172167 1050 840.0 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186172624 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186172624 1071 "Which of the following equations defines two parallel lines in the $x_{1}x_{2}$-plane?" 13194139536011 17592186045417 false] [17592186172624 1071 "Which of the following equations defines two parallel lines in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane?" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186172624 1074 "The correct answer is \n\n$$\n{C}_{4}\\,:\\,{x}_{2}^{2}=7.\n$$\n\nThis equation defines two parallel lines, ${x}_{2}=\\sqrt{7}$ and ${x}_{2}=-\\sqrt{7},$ in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane.\n\nNotice that the other equations do not define two parallel lines:\n\n- ${C}_{1}\\,:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=0$ defines a single point $(0,0)$ in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane.\n\n- ${C}_{2}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{9}=1$ is the equation of a hyperbola with semiaxes $2$ and $3.$\n\n- ${C}_{3}\\,:\\,{x}_{2}^{2}=2{x}_{1}$ is the equation of a parabola that opens in the direction of the positive ${x}_{1}$-axis.\n\n- ${C}_{5}\\,:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{4}=-1$ has no points in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane that satisfy the equation (imaginary ellipse)." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186173152 1064 "The general equation of a second order (quadratic) curve is an expression of the form\n\n$$\\alpha_{11}x_1^2+2\\alpha_{12}x_1x_2 + \\alpha_{22}x_2^2 + 2\\alpha_{13}x_1+ 2\\alpha_{23}x_2 +\\alpha_{33}=0,$$\n\nwhere $x_1$ and $x_2$ are variables, and $\\alpha_{ij} \\in \\Bbb R$ for all $1\\leq i,j \\leq 3.$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$3x_1^2-x_1x_2-5x_2^2+5x_2 - 1 = 0$$\n\nis a general quadratic curve." 13194139536011 17592186045417 false] [17592186173152 1064 "The general equation of a second order (quadratic) curve is an expression of the form \n\n$$\n\\alpha_{11}x_1^2+2\\alpha_{12}x_1x_2 + \\alpha_{22}x_2^2 + 2\\alpha_{13}x_1+ 2\\alpha_{23}x_2 +\\alpha_{33}=0,\n$$\n\nwhere  $x_1$ and $x_2$ are variables, and $\\alpha_{ij} \\in \\Bbb R$ for all $1\\leq i,j \\leq 3.$ \n\nFor example, \n\n$$\n3x_1^2-x_1x_2-5x_2^2+5x_2 - 1 = 0\n$$\n\nis a general quadratic curve." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186173153 1064 "In general, the cross-term of a quadratic equation can be eliminated by an orthogonal change of variables. That is, one of the kind \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} =P \\mathbf{y} ,\n$$\n\n  where the columns of $P$ form an orthonormal  eigenbasis for the corresponding quadratic form's matrix.\n\nLet $\\mathcal C$ be a plane curve defined as \n\n$$\n\\mathcal C:\\: 2x_1x_2 -2\\sqrt2x_1+2\\sqrt2x_2 =8.\n$$\n\nHow do we determine the type of our curve?\n\nNotice that the quadratic form $2x_1x_2$ can be reduced to canonical form $y_1^2-y_2^2$ using the change of variables\n![](images/ab/ab1ecd406bcaf2f48f60d0e97c214f1fae9ab911b08ebe2bd74a6e43e0aa524b.svg)\n\nFirst, let's write the given change of variables explicitly:\n\n![](images/18/1820ce650da02daa432023803a773821f63753e2adda1c6e55964fdddb913934.svg)\n\nWe know that this change of variables reduces the corresponding quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf x) = 2x_1x_2$ to its canonical form. Indeed, performing the change of variables on $\\mathcal C,$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1}+2\\sqrt{2}{x}_{2}=8\\,⟹\\,{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}+4{y}_{2}=8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n and, by completing the  square for the variable $y_2$,  we get the equation \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{2}-{({y}_{2}-2)}^{2} & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of the resulting equation in the $y_1,y_2$ variables is the following.\n\n![](images/d5/d532f5207a9d518f92024c86689285834b86c961d0f647d6ee99ad6dd9c7c329.png)\n\nNotice that we can write down the change-of-variables matrix as\n![](images/2d/2d128752c7b7a73db6a3dfc8c1fc232347b01d50376b46b08704a9ebd4e3ceb7.svg)\nwhich is a rotation matrix. Looking at the diagram above, we see that going from one coordinate system to another can be viewed as a rotation of our axes by $45^\\circ.$\n\n**Watch out!** In general, this action is an orthogonal transformation (it can be a rotation, a reflection, or a combination of such transformations).\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1}={y}_{1}, \\\\ {z}_{2}={y}_{2}-2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{2}-{({y}_{2}-2)}^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}-\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis (standard) equation represents a hyperbola in which  both semiaxes are equal to $2.$      \n\nIn the diagram below we represent the curve in the $z_1,z_2$ variables.\n\n![](images/c3/c36709f752bd9c319662e7e2150c54031cc8fb3934be37711c65e7a7668829ca.png)\n\nLooking at the diagram above, we see that in the final step we simply translated our system by $(0,2)$ in $y$-coordinates.\n\n**Note:** Finding the simplest equation of a quadratic curve can be viewed as the following series of steps:\n\n- **Step 1**. We eliminate the cross-term by reducing the quadratic part to canonical form. Geometrically, this is equivalent to an orthogonal transformation of our coordinate system.\n\n- **Step 2**. We eliminate the linear terms by completing the squares. Geometrically, this is equivalent to a translation of our coordinate system." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186173153 1064 "In general, the cross-term of a quadratic equation can be eliminated by an orthogonal change of variables. That is, one of the kind $$\\mathbf{x} =P \\mathbf{y} ,$$ where the columns of $P$ form an orthonormal eigenbasis for the corresponding quadratic form's matrix.\n\nLet $\\mathcal C$ be a plane curve defined as\n\n$$\\mathcal C:\\: 2x_1x_2 -2\\sqrt2x_1+2\\sqrt2x_2 =8.$$\n\nHow do we determine the type of our curve?\n\nNotice that the quadratic form $2x_1x_2$ can be reduced to canonical form $y_1^2-y_2^2$ using the change of variables $$\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.$$\n\nFirst, let's write the given change of variables explicitly:\n\n$$\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} &=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\\right.$$\n\nWe know that this change of variables reduces the corresponding quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf x) = 2x_1x_2$ to its canonical form. Indeed, performing the change of variables on $\\mathcal C,$ gives\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1}+2\\sqrt{2}{x}_{2}=8\\,⟹\\,{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}+4{y}_{2}=8,\\end{aligned}$$ and, by completing the square for the variable $y_2$, we get the equation\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{2}-{({y}_{2}-2)}^{2} & =4.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThe graph of the resulting equation in the $y_1,y_2$ variables is the following.\n\n![](images/d5/d532f5207a9d518f92024c86689285834b86c961d0f647d6ee99ad6dd9c7c329.png)\n\nNotice that we can write down the change-of-variables matrix as $$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}{45}^{∘} & -\\operatorname{sin}{45}^{∘} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}{45}^{∘} & \\operatorname{cos}{45}^{∘}\\end{bmatrix},$$ which is a rotation matrix. Looking at the diagram above, we see that going from one coordinate system to another can be viewed as a rotation of our axes by $45^\\circ.$\n\n**Watch out!** In general, this action is an orthogonal transformation (it can be a rotation, a reflection, or a combination of such transformations).\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1} &={y}_{1}, \\\\ {z}_{2} &={y}_{2}-2,\\end{aligned}\\right.$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{2}-{({y}_{2}-2)}^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}-\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThis (standard) equation represents a hyperbola in which both semiaxes are equal to $2.$\n\nIn the diagram below we represent the curve in the $z_1,z_2$ variables.\n\n![](images/c3/c36709f752bd9c319662e7e2150c54031cc8fb3934be37711c65e7a7668829ca.png)\n\nLooking at the diagram above, we see that in the final step we simply translated our system by $(0,2)$ in $y$-coordinates.\n\n**Note:** Finding the simplest equation of a quadratic curve can be viewed as the following series of steps:\n\n- **Step 1**. We eliminate the cross-term by reducing the quadratic part to canonical form. Geometrically, this is equivalent to an orthogonal transformation of our coordinate system.\n- **Step 2**. We eliminate the linear terms by completing the squares. Geometrically, this is equivalent to a translation of our coordinate system." 13194139536011 17592186045417 false] [17592186173155 1064 "A quadratic equation with no cross-terms can be rewritten in standard form by completing the square. Let's see how this is done in the following example.\n\nSuppose we are given the equation\n\n$$2x_1^2+x_2^2 - 4x_1 + 4x_2 = 2,$$\n\nwhat type of curve does it represent?\n\nFirst, we complete the squares for the variables $x_1$ and $x_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}-4{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} & =2 \\\\ 2({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1})+({x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{2}) & =2 \\\\ 2({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}+1-1)+({x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{2}+4-4) & =2 \\\\ 2({x}_{1}-1)^{2}-2+({x}_{2}+2)^{2}-4 & =2 \\\\ 2({x}_{1}-1)^{2}+({x}_{2}+2)^{2} & =8\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNow, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &={x}_{1}-1, \\\\ {y}_{2} &={x}_{2}+2,\\end{aligned}\\right.$$\n\nwe get the following equation in standard form:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2} & =8 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{8} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{8})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThis represents an ellipse with semiaxes $2$ and $\\sqrt{8}.$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 false] [17592186173155 1064 "A quadratic equation with no cross-terms can be rewritten in standard form by completing the square. Let's see how this is done in the following example. \n\nSuppose we are given the equation  \n\n$$\n2x_1^2+x_2^2 - 4x_1 + 4x_2 = 2,\n$$\n\nwhat type of curve does it represent?\n\nFirst, we complete the squares for the variables $x_1$ and $x_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}-4{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} & =2 \\\\ 2({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1})+({x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{2}) & =2 \\\\ 2({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}+1-1)+({x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{2}+4-4) & =2 \\\\ 2({x}_{1}-1)^{2}-2+({x}_{2}+2)^{2}-4 & =2 \\\\ 2({x}_{1}-1)^{2}+({x}_{2}+2)^{2} & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}={x}_{1}-1, \\\\ {y}_{2}={x}_{2}+2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the following equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2} & =8 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{8} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{8})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis represents an ellipse with semiaxes $2$ and $\\sqrt{8}.$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186173166 1064 "There are also a few so-called *degenerate (real) quadratic curves:*\n\n| Standard Equation | Description |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2}=0$ | Pair of\n intersecting  lines |\n| ${x_i^2}=a^2$ | Pair of \n parallel lines |\n| ${x_i^2}=0$ | Pair of \n coincident lines |\n| $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2}=0$ | Single point |\n\nLet's see why these equations define the described curves:\n\n- $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2}=0$ is equivalent to $(bx_1 - ax_2)(bx_1 + ax_2)=0$ which in turn gives equations of two lines $bx_1 - ax_2=0$ and $bx_1 + ax_2=0$ intersecting at the origin.\n\n- ${x_i^2}=a^2$ is equivalent to $(x_i - a)(x_i + a)=0$ which in turn gives equations of two parallel lines $x_i =a$ and $x_i=-a.$\n\n- ${x_i^2}=0$ gives equations of two coincident lines $x_i =0$ and $-x_i=0.$\n\n- $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2}=0$ is equivalent to $b^2x_1^2+a^2x_2^2=0$ which in turn gives a single point, the origin, since the sum of two squares is $0$ if and only if both squares are $0.$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186173166 1064 "There are also a few so-called *degenerate (real) quadratic curves:*\n\n| Standard Equation | Description |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2}=0$ | Pair of<br> intersecting lines |\n| ${x_i^2}=a^2$ | Pair of <br> parallel lines |\n| ${x_i^2}=0$ | Pair of <br> coincident lines |\n| $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2}=0$ | Single point |\n\nLet's see why these equations define the described curves:\n\n- $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2}=0$ is equivalent to $(bx_1 - ax_2)(bx_1 + ax_2)=0$ which in turn gives equations of two lines $bx_1 - ax_2=0$ and $bx_1 + ax_2=0$ intersecting at the origin.\n- ${x_i^2}=a^2$ is equivalent to $(x_i - a)(x_i + a)=0$ which in turn gives equations of two parallel lines $x_i =a$ and $x_i=-a.$\n- ${x_i^2}=0$ gives equations of two coincident lines $x_i =0$ and $-x_i=0.$\n- $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2}=0$ is equivalent to $b^2x_1^2+a^2x_2^2=0$ which in turn gives a single point, the origin, since the sum of two squares is $0$ if and only if both squares are $0.$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 false] [17592186173167 1064 "Finally, some quadratic equations might define an *empty set* (or so-called *imaginary curve*):\n\n| Standard Equation | Description |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2}=-1$ | Imaginary ellipse |\n| $x_i^2=-a^2$ | Pair of imaginary\n parallel lines |\n\nLet's see why these equations define the described curves:\n\n- Although there are no real solutions, the equation $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2} = -1,$ which is equivalent to $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{(a\\textrm{i})^2} + \\dfrac{x_2^2}{(b\\textrm{i})^2} = 1,$ has imaginary solutions, so we could interpret this as an imaginary ellipse.\n\n- Although there are no real solutions, the equation ${x_i^2}=-a^2$ is equivalent to $(x_i - a\\textrm{i})(x_i + a\\textrm{i})=0$ which in turn gives equations of two imaginary parallel lines $x_i =a\\textrm{i}$ and $x_i=-a\\textrm{i}.$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186173167 1064 "Finally, some quadratic equations might define an *empty set* (or so-called *imaginary curve*):\n\n| Standard Equation | Description |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2}=-1$ | Imaginary ellipse |\n| $x_i^2=-a^2$ | Pair of imaginary<br> parallel lines |\n\nLet's see why these equations define the described curves:\n\n- Although there are no real solutions, the equation $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2} = -1,$ which is equivalent to $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{(a\\textrm{i})^2} + \\dfrac{x_2^2}{(b\\textrm{i})^2} = 1,$ has imaginary solutions, so we could interpret this as an imaginary ellipse.\n- Although there are no real solutions, the equation ${x_i^2}=-a^2$ is equivalent to $(x_i - a\\textrm{i})(x_i + a\\textrm{i})=0$ which in turn gives equations of two imaginary parallel lines $x_i =a\\textrm{i}$ and $x_i=-a\\textrm{i}.$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 false] [17592186188056 1071 "Suppose the following random sample was drawn from the uniform distribution $U[0,1]$.\n$0.352, \\quad 0.567, \\quad 0.801$\n\nUse these numbers to generate three random numbers that follow a probability distribution with the CDF $F(x)$, given by\n${0,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 1, \\\\ 2 - \\frac{2}{x},\\end{vmatrix}1 \\le x \\le 2,; 1, \\mid x > 2$.\n\nRound your answers to $3$ decimal places." 13194139536011 17592186045417 false] [17592186188056 1071 "Suppose the following random sample was drawn from the uniform distribution $U[0,1].$ \n\n$$\n0.352, \\quad 0.567, \\quad 0.801\n$$\n\nUse these numbers to generate three random numbers that follow a probability distribution with the CDF $F(x),$ given by\n![](images/2c/2c4ac4b0cf243ba8b49024132642a3856210daa5a603ed534e636caef0083629.svg)\n\nRound your answers to $3$ decimal places." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186188056 1074 "Let the random variable $Y \\sim U[0,1]$ be uniformly distributed. Suppose that the function $F(x)$ is a cumulative distribution function, where\n\n$$\nF(a) = 0, F(b) = 1\n$$\n\nand $F$ is strictly monotonic on $a < x < b$. Then, the random variable $X$, given by\n\n$$\nX = F^{-1}(Y)\n$$\n\nis a continuous random variable with the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$.\n\nSo, we need to find the inverse of $y = F(x)$. To do this, we solve the following equation for $x$, taking into account that\n\n$$\n1 \\leq x \\leq 2\n$$\n\nand $0 \\lt y \\lt 1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 2 - \\frac{2}{x} \\\\\ny - 2 &= -\\frac{2}{x} \\\\\n2 - y &= \\frac{2}{x} \\\\\nx &= \\frac{2}{2 - y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the numbers drawn from $U[0,1]$ into the above equation:\n\n- For $y = 0.352$, we get $x = \\dfrac{2}{2 - 0.352} \\approx 1.214$\n- For $y = 0.567$, we get $x = \\dfrac{2}{2 - 0.567} \\approx 1.396$\n- For $y = 0.801$, we get $x = \\dfrac{2}{2 - 0.801} \\approx 1.668$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 false] [17592186188056 1074 "Let the random variable $Y \\sim U[0,1]$ be uniformly distributed. Suppose that the function $F(x)$ is a cumulative distribution function, where $F(a) = 0, F(b) = 1,$ and $F$ is strictly monotonic on $a <            x <            b.$ Then, the random variable $X,$ given by\n\n$$\nX = F^{-1}(Y)\n$$\n\nis a continuous random variable with the cumulative distribution function $F(x).$\n\nSo, we need to find the inverse of $y = F(x).$ To do this, we solve the following equation for $x,$ taking into account that $1 \\leq x \\leq 2$ and $0 \\lt y \\lt 1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2-\\frac{2}{x} \\\\ y-2 & =-\\frac{2}{x} \\\\ 2-y & =\\frac{2}{x} \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{2-y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the numbers drawn from $U[0,1]$ into the above equation:\n\n- For $y = 0.352,$ we get $x = \\dfrac{2}{2 - 0.352} \\approx 1.214$\n\n- For $y = 0.567,$ we get $x = \\dfrac{2}{2 - 0.567} \\approx 1.396$\n\n- For $y = 0.801,$ we get $x = \\dfrac{2}{2 - 0.801} \\approx 1.668$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186188060 1071 "Suppose the following random sample was drawn from the uniform distribution $U[0,1]$.\n$0.354, \\quad 0.593, \\quad 0.792$\nUse these numbers to generate three random numbers that follow a probability distribution with the PDF $f(x)$, given by\n${\\cos x,\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le \\frac{π}{2}, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$.\nRound your answers to $3$ decimal places." 13194139536011 17592186045417 false] [17592186188060 1071 "Suppose the following random sample was drawn from the uniform distribution $U[0,1].$ \n\n$$\n0.354, \\quad 0.593, \\quad 0.792\n$$\n\nUse these numbers to generate three random numbers that follow a probability distribution with the PDF $f(x),$ given by\n![](images/ea/ea0ee4d1fcf2e423ebd438e784160e525cb2486e91ba833cd84aae9ff0568344.svg)\nRound your answers to $3$ decimal places." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186188060 1074 "Let the random variable $Y \\sim U[0,1]$ be uniformly distributed. Suppose that the function $F(x)$ is a cumulative distribution function, where\n\n$$\nF(a) = 0, F(b) = 1\n$$\n\nand $F$ is strictly monotonic on $a < x < b$. Then, the random variable $X$, given by\n\n$$\nX = F^{-1}(Y)\n$$\n\nis a continuous random variable with the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$.\n\nFirst, we must find the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$ corresponding to our random variable. Since our PDF $f(x)$ is non-negative only for\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n{0,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 0, \\\\ \\int_{0}^{x}f(t)dt,\\end{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le \\frac{π}{2},; 1, \\mid x > \\frac{π}{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{x}f(t)dt &= \\int_{0}^{x}\\cos tdt \\\\\n&= \\left.\\sin t\\right|_{0}^{x} \\\\\n&= \\sin x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\n{0,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 0, \\\\ \\sin x,\\end{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le \\frac{π}{2},; 1, \\mid x > \\frac{π}{2}\n$$\n\nNext, we need to find the inverse of $y = F(x)$. So, we solve the following equation for $x$, taking into account that\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nand $0 \\lt y \\lt 1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= \\sin x \\\\\nx &= \\arcsin y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the numbers drawn from $U[0,1]$ into the above equation:\n\n- For $y = 0.354$, we get $x = \\arcsin(0.354) \\approx 0.362$\n- For $y = 0.593$, we get $x = \\arcsin(0.593) \\approx 0.635$\n- For $y = 0.792$, we get $x = \\arcsin(0.792) \\approx 0.914$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 false] [17592186188060 1074 "Let the random variable $Y \\sim U[0,1]$ be uniformly distributed. Suppose that the function $F(x)$ is a cumulative distribution function, where $F(a) = 0, F(b) = 1,$ and $F$ is strictly monotonic on $a <            x <            b.$ Then, the random variable $X,$ given by\n\n$$\nX = F^{-1}(Y)\n$$\n\nis a continuous random variable with the cumulative distribution function $F(x).$\n\nFirst, we must find the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$ corresponding to our random variable. Since our PDF $f(x)$ is non-negative only for $0 \\leq x \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2},$ we have\n![](images/53/535220c76d363aac546f42314de13394c1fd8b3001e95028a4215867ebc6ed41.svg)\n\nEvaluating the integral, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n![](images/8e/8eef1c7b3d8a69137cf7ac23ecf721751f43449d48b6f450256e15f501ee4c91.svg)\n\nNext, we need to find the inverse of $y = F(x).$ So, we solve the following equation for $x,$ taking into account that $0 \\leq x \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ and $0 \\lt y \\lt 1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the numbers drawn from $U[0,1]$ into the above equation:\n\n- For $y = 0.354,$ we get $x = \\arcsin(0.354) \\approx 0.362$\n\n- For $y = 0.593,$ we get $x = \\arcsin(0.593) \\approx 0.635$\n\n- For $y = 0.792,$ we get $x = \\arcsin(0.792) \\approx 0.914$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186188348 1038 #edb/ref 17592186557249 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186188348 1038 #edb/ref 17592186557298 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186188348 1038 #edb/ref 17592186557313 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186188348 1038 #edb/ref 17592186557321 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186188348 1038 #edb/ref 17592186557326 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186188352 1038 #edb/ref 17592186557240 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186188352 1038 #edb/ref 17592186557243 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186188352 1038 #edb/ref 17592186557254 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186188352 1038 #edb/ref 17592186557258 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186188352 1038 #edb/ref 17592186557295 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186188461 1050 480.0 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186189408 1064 "Most statistical computer packages have functionality that allows data from a particular probability distribution to be simulated. In this lesson, we'll learn how this process works.\n\nFor example, suppose we have a random variable with the cumulative distribution function\n\n$$\nF(x) = \\dfrac{1}{1+e^{-x}}\\,, \\qquad x \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=F(x)$ is shown below:\n\n![](images/f2/f26b4b7871d1f2b4c7a983986f596c4b599d24fbf34c87b443fb21025f26b5ad.png)\n\nSuppose we want to generate a sample of size $n=3$ from this distribution. To do this, we proceed as follows:\n\n- First, draw a random sample of size $n=3$ from the uniform distribution $Y \\sim U[0,1]$.\n- Then, find the values $x_i$ such that $y_i=F(x_i)$. In other words,\n$x_i=F^{-1}(y_i), \\qquad i=1,2,3$.\n\nNote that $F(x)$ is strictly increasing and therefore $F^{-1}(y)$ exists for all $y\\in (0,1)$.\n\nSuppose that we run our simulation and generate $n=3$ numbers from $Y\\sim U[0,1]$, and that our three numbers (rounded to $3$ decimal places) are\n\n$$\ny_1 = 0.214, \\qquad y_2 = 0.707, \\qquad y_3 = 0.819\n$$\n\nWe'll now use this data to generate three numbers $x_1, x_2$, and $x_3$ drawn from the distribution with CDF $F(x)$. The three $x_i$'s are the numbers shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/d9/d93db6abed9b1f227e5badb0d71868b1aee261d803af929e1fd8e45dc5f2932b.png)\n\nTo compute our $x_i$'s, we first need to find the inverse of $F(x)$. So we first write $y=F(x)$:\n\n$$\ny = \\frac{1}{1 + e^{-x}}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 + e^{-x} &= \\frac{1}{y} \\\\\ne^{-x} &= \\frac{1}{y} - 1 \\\\\ne^{-x} &= \\frac{1 - y}{y} \\\\\n- x &= \\ln (\\frac{1 - y}{y}) \\\\\nx &= -\\ln (\\frac{1 - y}{y}) \\\\\nx &= \\ln (\\frac{1 - y}{y})^{-1} \\\\\nx &= \\ln (\\frac{y}{1 - y})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0 < y < 1$.\n\nFinally, substituting the values $y_1,y_2,y_3$ into the formula for\n\n$$\nx=F^{-1}(y)\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\nx_1 \\approx -1.301, \\qquad x_2 \\approx 0.881, \\qquad x_3 \\approx 1.510\n$$\n\nThe algorithm above can be formalized in the following theorem:\n\n> *Let the random variable $Y \\sim U[0,1]$ be uniformly distributed. Suppose that the function $F(x)$ is a cumulative distribution function, where $F(a) = 0, F(b) = 1$, and $F$ is strictly monotonic on $a \\lt x \\lt b$, where $a$ and $b$ could be infinite. Then, the random variable $X$, given by $X = F^{-1}(Y)$ is a continuous random variable with the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$.*" 13194139536011 17592186045417 false] [17592186189408 1064 "Most statistical computer packages have functionality that allows data from a particular probability distribution to be simulated. In this lesson, we'll learn how this process works.\n\nFor example, suppose we have a random variable with the cumulative distribution function \n\n$$\nF(x) = \\dfrac{1}{1+e^{-x}}\\,, \\qquad x \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=F(x)$ is shown below:\n\n![](images/f2/f26b4b7871d1f2b4c7a983986f596c4b599d24fbf34c87b443fb21025f26b5ad.png)\n\n \n\nSuppose we want to generate a sample of size $n=3$ from this distribution. To do this, we proceed as follows:\n\n- First, draw a random sample of size $n=3$ from the uniform distribution $Y \\sim U[0,1].$\n\n- Then, find the values $x_i$ such that $y_i=F(x_i).$ In other words, \n\n$$\nx_i=F^{-1}(y_i), \\qquad i=1,2,3.\n$$\n\nNote that $F(x)$ is strictly increasing and therefore $F^{-1}(y)$ exists for all $y\\in (0,1).$ \n\nSuppose that we run our simulation and generate $n=3$ numbers from $Y\\sim U[0,1],$ and that our three numbers (rounded to $3$ decimal places) are \n\n$$\ny_1 = 0.214, \\qquad y_2 = 0.707, \\qquad y_3 = 0.819.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe'll now use this data to generate three numbers $x_1, x_2,$ and $x_3$ drawn from the distribution with CDF $F(x).$ The three $x_i$'s are the numbers shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/d9/d93db6abed9b1f227e5badb0d71868b1aee261d803af929e1fd8e45dc5f2932b.png)\n\nTo compute our $x_i$'s, we first need to find the inverse of $F(x).$ So we first write $y=F(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{1}{1+{e}^{-x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{e}^{-x} & =\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ {e}^{-x} & =\\frac{1}{y}-1 \\\\ {e}^{-x} & =\\frac{1-y}{y} \\\\ -x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1-y}{y}) \\\\ x & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1-y}{y}) \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡{(\\frac{1-y}{y})}^{-1} \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{y}{1-y}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0 < y < 1.$\n\nFinally, substituting the values $y_1,y_2,y_3$ into the formula for $x=F^{-1}(y),$ we obtain \n\n$$\nx_1 \\approx -1.301, \\qquad x_2 \\approx 0.881, \\qquad x_3 \\approx 1.510.\n$$\n\nThe algorithm above can be formalized in the following theorem:\n\n*Let the random variable $Y \\sim U[0,1]$ be uniformly distributed. Suppose that the function $F(x)$ is a cumulative distribution function, where $F(a) = 0, F(b) = 1,$ and $F$ is strictly monotonic on $a \\lt x \\lt b,$ where $a$ and $b$ could be infinite. Then, the random variable $X,$ given by \n\n$$\nX = F^{-1}(Y)\n$$\n\n is a continuous random variable with the cumulative distribution function $F(x).$*" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186228234 1075 #edb/ref 17592186228235 13194139536011 17592186045417 false] [17592186228234 1075 #edb/ref 17592186228236 13194139536011 17592186045417 false] [17592186228234 1075 #edb/ref 17592186228237 13194139536011 17592186045417 false] [17592186228234 1075 #edb/ref 17592186556717 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186228234 1075 #edb/ref 17592186556988 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186228234 1075 #edb/ref 17592186557029 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186228234 1078 #edb/ref 17592186556717 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186228238 1075 #edb/ref 17592186228239 13194139536011 17592186045417 false] [17592186228238 1075 #edb/ref 17592186228240 13194139536011 17592186045417 false] [17592186228238 1075 #edb/ref 17592186228241 13194139536011 17592186045417 false] [17592186228238 1075 #edb/ref 17592186228242 13194139536011 17592186045417 false] [17592186228238 1075 #edb/ref 17592186556439 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186228238 1075 #edb/ref 17592186556732 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186228238 1075 #edb/ref 17592186556867 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186228238 1075 #edb/ref 17592186556924 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186228238 1078 #edb/ref 17592186556439 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186229131 1038 #edb/ref 17592186557281 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186229131 1038 #edb/ref 17592186557289 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186229131 1038 #edb/ref 17592186557325 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186229675 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186229675 1071 "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x(1)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"160px\">$2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5(t-1))+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5(t-1))$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td><td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,+{\\int }_{1}^{t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"160px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,\\text{d}s$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td><td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">${e}^{5s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5(t-s))$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186229675 1071 "$x^{′}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x + \\begin{bmatrix}e^{5t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, x(1) = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: $e^{At} = \\begin{bmatrix}\\cosh (5t) & \\sinh (5t) \\\\ \\sinh (5t) & \\cosh (5t)\\end{bmatrix}$, where $A = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$.*\n\n| $x(t) =$ |  | $2\\cosh (5(t - 1)) + \\sinh (5(t - 1))$ |  | $+ \\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{t}$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $ds$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  | $e^{5s}\\sinh (5(t - s))$ |  |  |  |  |" 13194139536011 17592186045417 false] [17592186229675 1074 "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)={{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+{{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{⏟}}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}].$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-1)}\\,x(1) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5(t-1)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5(t-1)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5(t-1)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5(t-1))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5(t-1))+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5(t-1)) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5(t-1))+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5(t-1))\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5(t-s)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5(t-s))\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5(t-s)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5(t-s))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5s} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5s}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5(t-s)) \\\\ {e}^{5s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5(t-s))\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5(t-1))+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5(t-1)) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5(t-1))+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5(t-1))\\end{aligned}]+{\\int }_{1}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5s}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5(t-s)) \\\\ {e}^{5s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5(t-s))\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186231418 1038 #edb/ref 17592186557331 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186231419 1038 #edb/ref 17592186557307 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186281503 1074 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}z) \\\\  & =1+(-2)+{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}({x}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type I solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,y\\le z\\le {x}^{2}+y\\}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}-1\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,{x}^{2}-1\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186281503