7208 1075 #edb/ref 17592186556861 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557208 1075 #edb/ref 17592186556979 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557208 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557208 1077 "selection" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557208 1078 #edb/ref 17592186556536 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557208 1079 #uuid "ff339910-4fbb-5844-b100-4c1b4c7881f7" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557209 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557121 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557209 1069 "q-51867" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557209 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557209 1071 "Find the common difference of an arithmetic sequence if its seventh and twenty-second terms are $126$ and $-9$, respectively." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557209 1072 #uuid "028f9fde-1b16-58aa-96b4-26b7759a29ce" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557209 1074 "The first thing to note is that the terms are *decreasing*, and therefore we expect $d$ to be *negative*.\n\nWe are given the terms\n\n$$\n{a}_{7}=126,\\,{a}_{22}=-9.\n$$\n\nSo, to find $d,$ we apply the formula with $n=22$ and $m=7:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{n}-{a}_{m}}{n-m} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{22}-{a}_{7}}{22-7} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-9)-126}{22-7} \\\\  & =\\frac{-135}{15} \\\\  & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common difference is $d=-9.$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557210 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557067 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557210 1069 "q-71434" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557210 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557210 1071 "A container with $\\frac{3}{4}$ gallons of weed killer can spray $\\frac{7}{4}$ lawns. What is the unit rate of gallons per lawn?" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557210 1072 #uuid "06090a54-3bf7-5dce-b7f7-51954f025453" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557210 1074 "To compute the unit rate of gallons per lawn, we divide the number of gallons, $\\frac{3}{4},$ by the number of lawns, $\\frac{7}{4}$, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{4}÷\\frac{7}{4}\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}÷\\frac{7}{4} & =\\frac{3}{4}\\times \\frac{4}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\times 4}{4\\times 7} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\times \\cancel{4}}{\\cancel{4}\\times 7} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit rate is $\\frac{3}{7}$ gallons per lawn." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557211 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557160 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557211 1069 "q-277438" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557211 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557211 1071 "Let $f(x)=a\\sqrt{2x+b}+c.$ Now consider the following properties of$f(x):$\n\n- The domain of $f(x)$ is $x\\in [4,\\infty ).$\n\n- The range is $f(x)\\in [-1,\\infty ).$\n\n- The point $(6,3)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x).$\n\nTherefore, our function is\n$f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557211 1072 #uuid "0bb9e173-3e0e-5cad-b727-d2df13c7ef82" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557211 1074 "Let's consider each property in turn.\n\n- The domain of $f(x)=a\\sqrt{2x+b}+c\\,$ is determined by the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+b & \\ge 0 \\\\ 2x & \\ge -b \\\\ x & \\ge -\\frac{b}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that the domain is $x\\in [4,\\infty ),$ which is equivalent to $x\\ge 4.$ Hence, we obtain\n\n$$\n-\\frac{b}{2}=4\\,⟹\\,b=-8.\n$$\n\n- Since the range is unbounded on the right-hand side, we know that $a>0.$ Furthermore, since we are given that the lowest point in the range is $-1,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{a\\sqrt{2x-8}}_{⏟}}_{\\ge 0}+c\\ge -1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $c=-1.$\n\n- Finally, since the point $(6,3)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(6) & =3 \\\\ a\\sqrt{2(6)-8}-1 & =3 \\\\ 2a-1 & =3 \\\\ a & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our function is $f(x)=2\\sqrt{2x-8}-1.$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557212 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557135 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557212 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557141 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557212 1069 "q-223794" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557212 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557212 1071 "In **ascending order**, the solutions to $4{g}^{2}=\\frac{2g}{5}$ are $g=${{field-1}} and $g=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557212 1072 #uuid "0d0ef510-de0c-500a-8eb5-ba614cc6f88f" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557212 1074 "First, we move all of the terms to the left-hand side and then factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{g}^{2} & =\\frac{2g}{5} \\\\ 4{g}^{2}-\\frac{2g}{5} & =0 \\\\ 2g(2g-\\frac{1}{5}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the zero product rule to get\n\n$$\n2g=0\\,\\text{or}\\,2g-\\frac{1}{5}=0,\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\ng=0\\,\\text{or}\\,g=\\frac{1}{10}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $g=0$ and $g=\\frac{1}{10}.$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557213 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557183 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557213 1069 "q-40131" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557213 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557213 1071 "Find a matrix that defines the orthogonal projection of ${R}^{3}$ onto the subspace spanned by $a=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557213 1072 #uuid "196a5704-c0f5-5a90-b92b-f7fa820f51b0" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557213 1074 "Recall that the matrix ${P}_{S}=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}$ maps any vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ onto its orthogonal projection in the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of the matrix $A.$ \n\nIn our case, $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S} & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{([\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={6}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557214 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557148 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557214 1069 "q-71958" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557214 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557214 1071 "Given that $f(x)={(2x-11)}^{6},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557214 1072 #uuid "1b4d3073-0a5e-5d32-b8ba-a307535b4915" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557214 1074 "Let $g(x)=2x-11$ and $f(g(x))=g(x)^{6}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{6})^{′}=6{g}^{5}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(2x-11)^{′}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =6{g}^{5}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =12(2x-11)^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557215 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557099 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557215 1069 "q-11480" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557215 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557215 1071 "Consider the finite region bounded by the curve $y={x}^{2}-{x}^{3}$ and the $x$-axis. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557215 1072 #uuid "1d2ad0a0-cc2c-5c3a-a170-bcfe4847272e" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557215 1074 "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(1-x) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are  $x=0$ and  $x=1.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=1.$ \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}x({x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{3}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={2\\pi (\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}\\text{ }-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\pi [(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{5})-0] \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{1}{20}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557216 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557123 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557216 1069 "q-95938" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557216 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557216 1071 "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. You're given that the system of equations given below connects the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2}=\\frac{1}{6}{a}_{2}-3{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3}=\\frac{1}{5}{a}_{3}-{q}_{1}-2{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of ${r}_{11}+{r}_{13}+{r}_{33}?$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557216 1072 #uuid "220c02c6-9021-54d4-b05d-5f454f3a5b70" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557216 1074 "Let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2}=\\frac{1}{6}{a}_{2}-3{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3}=\\frac{1}{5}{a}_{3}-{q}_{1}-2{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}=4{q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}=18{q}_{1}+6{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3}=5{q}_{1}+10{q}_{2}+5{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, this system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 18 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{11}+{r}_{13}+{r}_{33}=4+5+5=14.\n$$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557217 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557111 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557217 1069 "q-18525" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557217 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557217 1071 "![](images/f9/f9738ccf12d8b9e8f3ed84870371972767e67a624f31fe5782f39679253965c4.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}.$ What is the value of $k?$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557217 1072 #uuid "228ddf28-6449-527a-a0f3-502e03cb4eba" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557217 1074 "To plot the graph of $y=k{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x},$ we take the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}$ and stretch it vertically by a scale factor of  $k.$ Under the action of this transformation, the $y$-intercept is mapped from $(0,1)$ to $(0,k).$\n\nComparing the point $(0,4)$ on the graph to $(0,k),$ we see that we must have $k=4.$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557218 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557127 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557218 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557188 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557218 1069 "q-274961" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557218 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557218 1071 "Determine the range of $f(x)=\\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt{x-3}+3.$\n\nThe range is $f(x)$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557218 1072 #uuid "24750a35-9998-5176-94af-1ab0c0c1ac7c" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557218 1074 "The range of $\\sqrt{x}$ is\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x}\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts do not affect the range. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x-3}\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $\\frac{3}{5}$ and then adding $3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x-3} & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt{x-3} & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt{x-3}+3 & \\ge 3 \\\\ f(x) & \\ge 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of the given function is $f(x)\\in [3,\\infty ).$\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/c2/c27b96134ab2d7004262d14163db2cd3714fd0c18fb6bb84444252d5bdac2ef7.png)" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557219 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557071 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557219 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557118 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557219 1069 "q-256858" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557219 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557219 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-{x}^{2}$ in the interval $[0.5,1].$ Calculate the first two iterations of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point ${x}_{0}=1.$ Round your answers to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,{x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557219 1072 #uuid "25247587-20e6-5dbb-bca0-455ff0ba3862" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557219 1074 "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-{x}^{2})^{′}=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)-2x, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)-2x)^{′}=-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4{x}_{n})-2{x}_{n})}{(-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{x}_{n})-2)}={x}_{n}-\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4{x}_{n})+{x}_{n}}{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{x}_{n})+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4{x}_{0})+{x}_{0}}{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{x}_{0})+1} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot 1)+1}{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot 1)+1} \\\\  & =0.878\\,555…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4{x}_{1})+{x}_{1}}{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{x}_{1})+1} \\\\  & =(0.878\\,555…)-\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(0.878\\,555…))+0.878\\,555…}{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(0.878\\,555…))+1} \\\\  & =0.901\\,873…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}≈0.879$ and ${x}_{2}≈0.902,$ rounded to three decimal places." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557220 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557072 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557220 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557136 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557220 1069 "q-334995" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557220 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557220 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-3x+5xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=5y-3xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the predators and $y=y(t)$ represents the prey. Find the nonzero equilibrium of the model.\n\n$(${{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557220 1072 #uuid "257d204a-c14e-5aa5-8045-bec1e0148274" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557220 1074 "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-3x+5xy=0 \\\\ 5y-3xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x+5xy & =0 \\\\ x(-3+5y) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x=0$ or $y=\\frac{3}{5}.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $x=0,$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5y-3(0)y=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, one equilibrium is $(0,0).$\n\n- If $y=\\frac{3}{5},$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5y-3xy & =0 \\\\ y(5-3x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}(5-3x) & =0 \\\\ 5-3x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(\\frac{5}{3},\\frac{3}{5}).$\n\nTherefore, the nonzero equilibrium of the model is\n$(\\frac{5}{3},\\frac{3}{5}).$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557221 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557065 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557221 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557076 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557221 1069 "q-223742" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557221 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557221 1071 "In **ascending order**, the solutions to $\\frac{1}{5}{t}^{2}=-3t$ are $t=${{field-1}} and $t=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557221 1072 #uuid "267a0513-367c-516d-9933-b0be78b78e58" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557221 1074 "First, we move all of the terms to the left-hand side and then factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}{t}^{2} & =-3t \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}{t}^{2}+3t & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}t(t+15) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the zero product rule to get\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{5}t=0\\,\\text{or}\\,t+15=0,\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\nt=0\\,\\text{or}\\,t=-15.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $t=-15$ and $t=0.$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557222 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557091 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557222 1069 "q-95396" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557222 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557222 1071 "Consider the change of variables given by the matrix equation\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand the curve $C,$ defined as\n\n$$\nC:\\,24{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+21{x}_{2}^{2}-\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2}=-\\frac{5}{16}.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that the change of variables above reduces $Q(x)=24{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+21{x}_{2}^{2}$ to its canonical (diagonal) form $25{y}_{1}^{2}+20{y}_{2}^{2},$ what type of a quadratic curve is defined by $C?$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557222 1072 #uuid "27762923-ddbc-55ca-b261-ca5c61feb064" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557222 1074 "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\,{{24{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+21{x}_{2}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}-\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2}=-\\frac{5}{16}\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+21{x}_{2}^{2}-\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2} & =-\\frac{5}{16} \\\\ 25{y}_{1}^{2}+20{y}_{2}^{2}-\\sqrt{5}(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2})+2\\sqrt{5}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2}) & =-\\frac{5}{16} \\\\ 25{y}_{1}^{2}+20{y}_{2}^{2}+5{y}_{2} & =-\\frac{5}{16} \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{2}^{2}+{y}_{2} & =-\\frac{1}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable ${y}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{2}^{2}+{y}_{2} & =-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}+4({y}_{2}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{2}) & =-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}+4({y}_{2}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{2}+\\frac{1}{64}-\\frac{1}{64}) & =-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}+4({y}_{2}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{2}+\\frac{1}{64})-\\frac{4}{64} & =-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}+4({y}_{2}+\\frac{1}{8})^{2}-\\frac{1}{16} & =-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}+4({y}_{2}+\\frac{1}{8})^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1}={y}_{1}, \\\\ {z}_{2}={y}_{2}+\\frac{1}{8},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{z}_{1}^{2}+4{z}_{2}^{2} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{\\frac{1}{5}}+\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{\\frac{1}{4}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}})^{2}}+\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{(\\frac{1}{2})^{2}} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nA sum of squares is equal to zero if and only if each summand is zero, so the given quadratic equation represents a single point." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557223 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557176 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557223 1069 "q-112034" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557223 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557223 1071 "Solve the equation $6{w}^{2}+3w-5=2{w}^{2}-5.$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557223 1072 #uuid "27e28aad-c990-5820-b03a-4018644801a7" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557223 1074 "First, we move all of the terms to the left-hand side and then factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{w}^{2}+3w-5 & =2{w}^{2}-5 \\\\ 6{w}^{2}+3w-5-(2{w}^{2}-5) & =0 \\\\ 6{w}^{2}+3w-5-2{w}^{2}+5 & =0 \\\\ (6{w}^{2}-2{w}^{2})+3w+(-5+5) & =0 \\\\ 4{w}^{2}+3w & =0 \\\\ w(4w+3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the zero product rule to get\n\n$$\nw=0\\,\\text{or}\\,4w+3=0,\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\nw=0\\,\\text{or}\\,w=-\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $w=-\\frac{3}{4},0.$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557224 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557154 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557224 1069 "q-394147" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557224 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557224 1071 "Given that the function $f(x)={x}^{6}-12x$ has a critical point in the interval $[1,2],$ use the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from ${x}_{0}=1.5$ to estimate the critical point. Round your final answer to two decimal places." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557224 1072 #uuid "2b6cfb99-55dc-52df-beff-19bcc94447aa" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557224 1074 "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{6}-12x)^{′}=6{x}^{5}-12, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(6{x}^{5}-12)^{′}=30{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{6{x}_{n}^{5}-12}{30{x}_{n}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(30{x}_{n}^{4})-(6{x}_{n}^{5}-12)}{30{x}_{n}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{24{x}_{n}^{5}+12}{30{x}_{n}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{24{x}_{0}^{5}+12}{30{x}_{0}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{24(1.5)^{5}+12}{30(1.5)^{4}} \\\\  & =1.279\\,012…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{24{x}_{1}^{5}+12}{30{x}_{1}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{24(1.279\\,012…)^{5}+12}{30(1.279\\,012…)^{4}} \\\\  & =1.172\\,682…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{24{x}_{2}^{5}+12}{30{x}_{2}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{24(1.172\\,682…)^{5}+12}{30(1.172\\,682…)^{4}} \\\\  & =1.149\\,659…. \\\\ {x}_{4} & =\\frac{24{x}_{3}^{5}+12}{30{x}_{3}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{24(1.149\\,659…)^{5}+12}{30(1.149\\,659…)^{4}} \\\\  & =1.148\\,699….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{3}≈1.15$ and ${x}_{4}≈1.15$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the critical point of the function is $x≈1.15,$ rounded to two decimal places." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557225 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557082 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557225 1069 "q-89802" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557225 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557225 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of $y=-\\sqrt{1-x}-1?$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557225 1072 #uuid "2cdfaece-91e9-59d5-8fba-ab96089ab7a5" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557225 1074 "First, let's re-write the expression, as follows:\n\n$$\ny=-\\sqrt{1-x}-1=-\\sqrt{-(x-1)}-1\n$$\n\nTo get the graph of $y=-\\sqrt{-(x-1)}-1,$ we can do the following transformations:\n\n- Take the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\n- Reflect the graph in the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=\\sqrt{-x}.$\n\nThe result is the solid blue curve shown below.\n\n![](images/85/854a720d691342b87a7f390176cdc60cf5d68d8863a755fed8929dbd3fecd655.png)\n\n- Reflect the graph in the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=-\\sqrt{-x}.$\n\nThe result is the solid blue curve shown below.\n\n![](images/b5/b5c145b598e3311edf3f9f6958245bd7a6b5a875e476d2f383e7c1c394f80538.png)\n\n- Translate the result to the right by $1$ unit to get the graph of $y=-\\sqrt{-(x-1)}.$\n\n- Translate the result down by $1$ unit to get the graph of $y=-\\sqrt{-(x-1)}-1.$\n\nThe result is the solid blue curve shown below.\n\n![](images/7a/7a7c548b2d93636bb00902b62a37fd75fc7aceea00c71574d7761b46775eb844.png)" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557226 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557130 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557226 1069 "q-102017" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557226 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557226 1071 "Given that a geometric sequence has the first term ${a}_{1}=-0.125$ and the $4$th term ${a}_{4}=-8$, what is the index of the term $-32?$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557226 1072 #uuid "2d4a048a-e911-5aea-a6de-6f7f20420447" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557226 1074 "Since we are given that ${a}_{4}=-8$, we can use the formula for the $n$th term to solve for $r$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ -8 & =-0.125\\cdot {r}^{4-1} \\\\ 64 & ={r}^{3} \\\\ {4}^{3} & ={r}^{3} \\\\ 4 & =r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n}=-0.125\\cdot {4}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for the value of $n$ that gives ${a}_{n}=-32,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-32 & =-0.125\\cdot {4}^{n-1} \\\\ \\frac{-32}{-0.125} & ={4}^{n-1} \\\\ 256 & ={4}^{n-1} \\\\ {4}^{4} & ={4}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nEquating the exponents gives:\n\n$$\n4=n-1\\,⟹\\,n=5\n$$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557227 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557178 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557227 1069 "q-169441" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557227 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557227 1071 "Owen takes $\\frac{4}{5}$ hours to do $24$ math exercises. How many exercises can he do in $1$ hour?" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557227 1072 #uuid "2fa5b801-b137-5833-adf3-4b3d17f99da2" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557227 1074 "To find the number of exercises Owen can do in $1$ hour, we compute the unit rate of exercises per hour. We do this by dividing the number of exercises Owen does, $24,$ by the number of hours, $\\frac{4}{5},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n24÷\\frac{4}{5}\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by a whole number is equivalent to multiplying the fraction by the reciprocal of that whole number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24÷\\frac{4}{5} & =24\\times \\frac{5}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{1}\\times \\frac{5}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{24\\times 5}{1\\times 4} \\\\  & =\\frac{120}{4} \\\\  & =30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Owen can do $30$ exercises in $1$ hour." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557228 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557073 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557228 1069 "q-82668" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557228 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557228 1071 "If $g(x)$ is given by the table shown below, what are its roots?\n\n| $x$ | $10$ | $20$ | $30$ | $40$ | $50$ | $60$ | $70$ | $80$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g(x)$ | $5$ | $15$ | $25$ | $0$ | $-15$ | $-25$ | $0$ | $5$ |" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557228 1072 #uuid "30380421-4837-53f2-affd-65b4ad08f0c9" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557228 1074 "The roots of the function are the values of $x$ such that $g(x)=0.$ In the given table, we see that $g(40)=0$ and $g(70)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the roots of the function are $x=40$ and $x=70.$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557229 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557084 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557229 1069 "q-115542" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557229 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557229 1071 "Let $f(x)=a\\sqrt{b-3x}.$ Now consider the following properties of $f(x):$\n\n- The domain of $f(x)$ is $x\\in (-\\infty ,1].$\n\n- The point $(-11,-24)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x).$\n\nFind the value of $a+b.$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557229 1072 #uuid "31d9f406-b6d6-5a07-bd60-36de4e4c1d5a" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557229 1074 "Let's consider each property in turn.\n\n- The domain of $f(x)=a\\sqrt{b-3x}\\,$ is determined by the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b-3x & \\ge 0 \\\\ -3x & \\ge -b \\\\ x & \\le \\frac{b}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that the domain is $x\\in (-\\infty ,1],$ which is equivalent to $x\\le 1.$ Hence, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{b}{3}=1\\,⟹\\,b=3.\n$$\n\n- Since the point $(-11,-24)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-11) & =a\\sqrt{3-3\\cdot (-11)} \\\\ -24 & =6a \\\\ a & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our function is $f(x)=-4\\sqrt{3-3x}$ and\n\n$$\na+b=-4+3=-1.\n$$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557230 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557125 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557230 1069 "q-109145" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557230 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557230 1071 "Consider the arc of the curve $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ that lies between $y=0$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557230 1072 #uuid "325cb3ef-db56-5f90-98ae-212e45702d0f" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557230 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${y}_{1}=0$ to ${y}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}y\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557231 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557167 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557231 1069 "q-67896" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557231 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557231 1071 "At a fair, a carousel makes $3$ revolutions in  $\\frac{3}{5}$ minutes. How many revolutions does it make in $1$ minute?" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557231 1072 #uuid "33c6a696-2687-53cf-91b5-ae5c51099777" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557231 1074 "To find out the number of revolutions the carousel makes in $1$ minute, we can compute the unit rate of revolutions per minute. We do this by dividing the number of revolutions, $3,$ by the number of minutes, $\\frac{3}{5}$, as follows:  \n\n$$\n3÷\\frac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nTo divide by a fraction, we multiply by the fraction's reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3÷\\frac{3}{5} & =3\\times \\frac{5}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{1}\\times \\frac{5}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\times 5}{1\\times 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{3} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the carousel makes  $5$ revolutions in $1$ minute." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557232 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557092 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557232 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557155 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557232 1069 "q-277276" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557232 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557232 1071 "Determine the domain of $f(x)=4\\sqrt{7+x}+1.$\n\nThe domain is $x\\in [${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557232 1072 #uuid "363ec8e4-c2ba-5e32-b1d7-37d1b114d1fa" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557232 1074 "The domain of $y=\\sqrt{x}$ is\n\n$$\nx\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the domain of $f(x)=4\\sqrt{7+x}+1,$ we replace $x$ with $7+x$ in the above inequality. This gives\n\n$$\n7+x\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7+x & \\ge 0 \\\\ x & \\ge -7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $y=f(x)$ is $x\\in [-7,\\infty ).$\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/1b/1b5b24146a009936bb2fab85afa4f40b888881e4d6978fb76e76e7180b2cd83d.png)" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557233 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557202 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557233 1069 "q-67957" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557233 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557233 1071 "A skydiver falls $\\frac{4}{5}$ of a mile in $\\frac{2}{5}$ minutes. What is the unit rate of miles fallen per minute?" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557233 1072 #uuid "37159145-3e5f-53f2-822d-4367df7cb7bc" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557233 1074 "To compute the unit rate of miles per minute, we divide the number of miles, $\\frac{4}{5},$ by the number of minutes, $\\frac{2}{5}$, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{5}÷\\frac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5}÷\\frac{2}{5} & =\\frac{4}{5}\\times \\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\times 5}{5\\times 2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\times 2\\times 5}{5\\times 2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{2}\\times \\cancel{5}\\times 2}{\\cancel{2}\\times \\cancel{5}} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit rate is $2$ miles per minute." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557234 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557070 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557234 1069 "q-279985" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557234 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557234 1071 "![](images/06/062562ff86719e1ea820642d424ac93705eb56f3ec9f49adbd09108735d4e284.png)\n\nThe graph above is a result of the reflection and translation of $y=\\sqrt{x}.$ Identify the equation that generated the graph.\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557234 1072 #uuid "3a8011cb-eb86-5ec9-8024-d1a5101b449a" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557234 1074 "To obtain the given graph, we can perform the following transformations:\n\n- Take the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\n- Reflect the graph in the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=\\sqrt{-x}.$\n\nThe result is the solid blue curve shown below.\n\n![](images/30/30add2e841bb25faa97692ec22309482c9b18d8c55f878057978f0896f7420ea.png)\n\n- Reflect the graph in the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=-\\sqrt{-x}.$\n\nThe result is the solid blue curve shown below.\n\n![](images/0b/0bab48c1fe5e6d1a0d90b1aae3c8cd123327d1b2e18ca8101e056726b76de002.png)\n\n- Translate the result to the right by $1$ units to get the graph of $y=-\\sqrt{-(x-1)}.$\n\n- Translate the result up by $4$ units to get the graph of $y=-\\sqrt{-(x-1)}+4.$\n\nThe result is the solid blue curve shown below.\n\n![](images/57/572a818b9621f8cec26f815429ff6d6e2846686afa348c7ae5a767dbd42fd3f6.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y=-\\sqrt{-(x-1)}+4.$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557235 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557186 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557235 1069 "q-112145" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557235 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557235 1071 "Given that $(x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x)={x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-3x+18,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557235 1072 #uuid "3aad7acd-4801-580e-a95b-e6983a14c705" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557235 1074 "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x+2)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -2 & 1 & -4 & -3 & 18 \\\\  &  & -2 & 12 & -18 \\\\  & 1 & -6 & 9 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{f(x)}{x+2}={x}^{2}-6x+9\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+2)({x}^{2}-6x+9).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+2)(x-3)(x-3)=(x+2)(x-3)^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have the cubic in factored form, we can tell that it has a single root at $x=-2$ and a double root at $x=3.$\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(0+2)(0-3)^{2} \\\\  & =(2)(-3)^{2} \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,18),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=-2,$ and\n\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=3.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/26/266609da7c307b98b8d5f280c75f6d8fd557c23b2e2e1885be37fa4ee3d7b229.png)" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557236 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557190 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557236 1069 "q-89991" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557236 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557236 1071 "Find the root of $f(x)=\\sqrt{3x+1}+4.$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557236 1072 #uuid "3b5454cb-d7d1-50b2-8aa5-cfa1e1b3686a" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557236 1074 "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3x+1}+4=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3x+1}+4 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{3x+1} & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any solutions since its left-hand side is always nonnegative, while the right-hand side is negative.\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has no roots." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557237 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557119 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557237 1069 "q-4640" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557237 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557237 1071 "![](images/1c/1c6079401f61658b450e105b113bea6e979040dbdcdd53a954479809480c2c03.png)\n\n$\\operatorname{csc}⁡B=$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557237 1072 #uuid "3cfa23a7-bca9-5408-b04d-471de7fe17c9" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557237 1074 "We compute the cosecant of the angle $B$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡B=\\frac{\\text{hypotenuse}}{\\text{opposite}}=\\frac{17}{8}\n$$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557238 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557094 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557238 1069 "q-162577" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557238 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557238 1071 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=3,$ $y=-1,$ $y=2,$ $z=0,$ $z=4.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=y{e}^{x}\\,i+{z}^{2}\\,j+3z\\,k,$ the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557238 1072 #uuid "3d423292-2025-559a-9094-6ebc6fb41834" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557238 1074 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y{e}^{x})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({z}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3z) \\\\  & =y{e}^{x}+0+3 \\\\  & =y{e}^{x}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}(y{e}^{x}+3)\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\,y{e}^{x}+3\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557239 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557203 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557239 1069 "q-56557" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557239 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557239 1071 "Given that a positive geometric sequence has the first term ${a}_{1}=2$ and the $3$rd term ${a}_{3}=8$, what is the index of the term $48?$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557239 1072 #uuid "3e8e9f1e-2957-5c38-a228-d5c88729f321" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557239 1074 "Since we are given that ${a}_{3}=8$, we can use the formula for the $n$th term to solve for $r$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ 8 & =2\\cdot {r}^{3-1} \\\\ 4 & ={r}^{2} \\\\ {2}^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ 2 & =r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are given that the sequence is positive (meaning all its terms must be positive numbers), the common ratio must be positive, and we discard the negative solution. Therefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =2\\cdot {2}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={2}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for the value of $n$ that gives ${a}_{n}=48,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}48 & ={2}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nHowever, $48$ is not a power of $2,$ so there is no integer solution to the equation above. Therefore, $48$ is not a term in the geometric sequence." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557240 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557126 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557240 1069 "q-147698" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557240 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557240 1071 "Suppose the following random sample was drawn from the uniform distribution $U[0,1].$ \n\n$$\n0.209,\\,0.493,\\,0.826\n$$\n\nUse these numbers to generate three random numbers that follow a probability distribution with the PDF $f(x),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x},\\, & 1\\le x\\le e, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRound your answers to $3$ decimal places." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557240 1072 #uuid "3ef6d345-db21-5f3a-aad1-2b5e56cc5e56" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557240 1074 "Let the random variable $Y∼U[0,1]$ be uniformly distributed. Suppose that the function $F(x)$ is a cumulative distribution function, where $F(a)=0,F(b)=1,$ and $F$ is strictly monotonic on $a<x<b.$ Then, the random variable $X,$ given by\n\n$$\nX={F}^{-1}(Y)\n$$\n\nis a continuous random variable with the cumulative distribution function $F(x).$\n\nFirst, we must find the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$ corresponding to our random variable. Since our PDF $f(x)$ is non-negative only for $1\\le x\\le e,$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & \\,x<1, \\\\ {\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & \\,1\\le x\\le e, \\\\ 1, & \\,x>e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{x}\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,{|}_{1}^{x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & \\,x<1, \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x, & \\,1\\le x\\le e, \\\\ 1, & \\,x>e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we need to find the inverse of $y=F(x).$ So, we solve the following equation for $x,$ taking into account that $1\\le x\\le e$ and $0<y<1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ x & ={e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the numbers drawn from $U[0,1]$ into the above equation:\n\n- For $y=0.209,$ we get $x={e}^{0.209}≈1.232$\n\n- For $y=0.493,$ we get $x={e}^{0.493}≈1.637$\n\n- For $y=0.826,$ we get $x={e}^{0.826}≈2.284$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557241 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557182 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557241 1069 "q-115158" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557241 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557241 1071 "![](images/a9/a9c9324b8b15e38c1037d39a744a9e1d28796ea70a5d300563f9eaaaef966522.png)\n\nIdentify the equation that generated the graph shown above." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557241 1072 #uuid "41c06b75-0eb3-5c66-92c4-5d80f2f59bde" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557241 1074 "To obtain the given graph, we perform the following transformations:\n\n- Take the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\n- Translate the graph down by $5$ units to get the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x}-5.$\n\nThe result is the solid blue curve shown below.\n\n![](images/f0/f005ef005ce79b953aa61c01266ab4b9ba39c215169faf833bd91529f0e99c77.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y=\\sqrt{x}-5.$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557242 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557192 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557242 1069 "q-63979" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557242 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557242 1071 "What is the index of the term $486$ for the geometric sequence below?\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=3{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=2,\\,n\\ge 1\n$$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557242 1072 #uuid "4274dfd7-9570-5881-a7a2-35e55995174b" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557242 1074 "This geometric sequence is given as a recurrence relation, i.e.,\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=r\\cdot {a}_{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=2,\\,r=3.\n$$\n\nWe're interested in finding $n$ such that ${a}_{n}=486.$ Using the formula for the $n$th term, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ 486 & =2\\cdot {3}^{n-1} \\\\ \\frac{486}{2} & ={3}^{n-1} \\\\ 243 & ={3}^{n-1} \\\\ {3}^{5} & ={3}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the exponents gives:\n\n$$\n5=n-1\\,⟹\\,n=6\n$$\n\nTherefore, $486$ is the $6$th term of the sequence." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557243 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557204 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557243 1069 "q-147745" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557243 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557243 1071 "Suppose the following random sample was drawn from the uniform distribution $U[0,1].$ \n\n$$\n0.691,\\,0.741,\\,0.839\n$$\n\nUse these numbers to generate three random numbers that follow a probability distribution with the PDF $f(x),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRound your answers to $3$ decimal places." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557243 1072 #uuid "483ad5dd-d09b-5c15-bb9e-2524c193114e" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557243 1074 "Let the random variable $Y∼U[0,1]$ be uniformly distributed. Suppose that the function $F(x)$ is a cumulative distribution function, where $F(a)=0,F(b)=1,$ and $F$ is strictly monotonic on $a<x<b.$ Then, the random variable $X,$ given by\n\n$$\nX={F}^{-1}(Y)\n$$\n\nis a continuous random variable with the cumulative distribution function $F(x).$\n\nFirst, we must find the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$ corresponding to our random variable. Since our PDF $f(x)$ is non-negative only for $0\\le x\\le 1,$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & \\,x<0, \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 1, & \\,x>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{x}2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & \\,x<0, \\\\ {x}^{2}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 1, & \\,x>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we need to find the inverse of $y=F(x).$ So, we solve the following equation for $x,$ taking into account that $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0<y<1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={x}^{2} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the numbers drawn from $U[0,1]$ into the above equation:\n\n- For $y=0.691,$ we get $x=\\sqrt{0.691}≈0.831$\n\n- For $y=0.741,$ we get $x=\\sqrt{0.741}≈0.861$\n\n- For $y=0.839,$ we get $x=\\sqrt{0.839}≈0.916$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557244 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557193 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557244 1069 "q-27526" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557244 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557244 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{2}{(\\frac{1}{8})}^{x}?$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557244 1072 #uuid "487e5162-7f53-555d-bd23-288509fce232" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557244 1074 "First we plot the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{8})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/f5/f5d15cae5283eb02d555bee57f399dd00c58ec319413ca5817b1edba1ac69fb9.png)\n\nThen, we scale this graph by a factor of $\\frac{1}{2}.$ The $y$-intercept becomes $(0,\\frac{1}{2}),$ and the resulting graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/1b/1b8e997035826ffcb6969c5bbdda46e256234fb5720f45aa281719be631929d4.png)" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557245 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557173 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557245 1069 "q-25402" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557245 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557245 1071 "Find all the values of $x$ that satisfy $2x-5>1$ or $2x-7<13.$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557245 1072 #uuid "49cc684c-6a61-5a0a-9979-944836444765" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557245 1074 "We start by solving $2x-5>1:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-5 & >1 \\\\ 2x & >6 \\\\ x & >3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/20/2048d360b427cfd94e92a16f7b9f8cd9ca9c88e808bf3b6205d60eebd71aa1cf.png)\n\nThen, we solve $2x-7<13:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-7 & <13 \\\\ 2x & <20 \\\\ x & <10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/c1/c1a63d1c991f309dbc10a89f0416c910c2c31bde5fdf069682a6127a1f66987a.png)\n\nTo graph the values of $x$ that satisfy either inequality, we overlay the two graphs on the same number line:\n\n![](images/e0/e03d04e5d2d5d571f5b80353081253087f99b2bf58fd86ae56eff3af26415b92.png)\n\nTherefore, the final solution is $x<10$ or $x>3.$ We can write this using interval notation as $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557246 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557171 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557246 1069 "q-95342" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557246 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557246 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${A}^{T}=A$\n\nII. ${A}^{2}=A$\n\nIII. $A$ is an orthogonal projection matrix" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557246 1072 #uuid "4aff059c-8353-530a-ad6d-89d8d7db0136" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557246 1074 "Recall that $Q$ is an orthogonal projection matrix if and only if ${Q}^{2}=Q$ and ${Q}^{T}=Q.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false since ${A}^{T}\\ne A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false since not both statements I and II are true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \" II only.\"" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557247 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557102 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557247 1069 "q-32571" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557247 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557247 1071 "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 5\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557247 1072 #uuid "4c02183d-b4b3-51bb-97f0-443673f54a1a" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557247 1074 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{32}=-\\frac{3}{2},{u}_{33}=-1,$ and ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=\\frac{3}{2}.$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557248 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557142 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557248 1069 "q-115385" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557248 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557248 1071 "What is the range of $f(x)=3\\sqrt{5x+9}?$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557248 1072 #uuid "4cbeb52a-0392-5f34-918d-f2e44920e406" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557248 1074 "The range of $\\sqrt{x}$ is\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x}\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches do not affect the range. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5x+9}\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5x+9} & \\ge 0 \\\\ 3\\sqrt{5x+9} & \\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of the given function is $f(x)\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/8e/8ece70a3c8855e00a0232248cb922a40c8c9756ef67ea12b012eb719c8ee3bd1.png)" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557249 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557168 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557249 1069 "q-147601" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557249 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557249 1071 "Suppose the following random sample was drawn from the uniform distribution $U[0,1].$ \n\n$$\n0.321,\\,0.567,\\,0.804\n$$\n\nUse these numbers to generate three random numbers that follow a probability distribution with the CDF $F(x),$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({e}^{x}),\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nRound your answers to $3$ decimal places." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557249 1072 #uuid "4e37507d-c5f2-5123-ae55-8166bf59d6ea" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557249 1074 "Let the random variable $Y∼U[0,1]$ be uniformly distributed. Suppose that the function $F(x)$ is a cumulative distribution function, where $F(a)=0,F(b)=1,$ and $F$ is strictly monotonic on $a<x<b.$ Then, the random variable $X,$ given by\n\n$$\nX={F}^{-1}(Y)\n$$\n\nis a continuous random variable with the cumulative distribution function $F(x).$\n\nSo, we need to find the inverse of $y=F(x).$ To do this, we solve the following equation for $x,$ taking into account that $0\\le y\\le 1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({e}^{x}) \\\\ \\frac{y\\pi }{2} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({e}^{x}) \\\\ {e}^{x} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{y\\pi }{2}) \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{y\\pi }{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the numbers drawn from $U[0,1]$ into the above equation:\n\n- For $y=0.321,$ we get $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{0.321\\pi }{2}))≈-0.595$\n\n- For $y=0.567,$ we get $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{0.567\\pi }{2}))≈0.212$\n\n- For $y=0.804,$ we get $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{0.804\\pi }{2}))≈1.146$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557250 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557153 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557250 1069 "q-95870" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557250 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557250 1071 "Which of the following equations defines a parabola that opens in the direction of the positive ${x}_{1}$-axis,  in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane?" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557250 1072 #uuid "4e624380-04d1-5614-ba5a-b0ac9bddf8d7" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557250 1074 "The correct answer is \n\n$$\n{C}_{3}\\,:\\,3{x}_{2}^{2}={x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis equation defines a parabola that opens in the direction of the positive ${x}_{1}$-axis.\n\nNotice that the other equations do not define a parabola:\n\n- ${C}_{1}\\,:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=0$ defines a single point $(0,0)$ in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane.\n\n- ${C}_{2}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{9}=1$ is the equation of a hyperbola with semiaxes $2$ and $3.$\n\n- ${C}_{4}\\,:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}=4$ represents two parallel lines in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane: ${x}_{1}=2$ and ${x}_{1}=-2.$\n\n- ${C}_{5}\\,:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{4}=-1$ has no points in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane that satisfy the equation (imaginary ellipse)." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557251 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557200 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557251 1069 "q-394148" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557251 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557251 1071 "Given that the function $f(x)={e}^{x}-3{x}^{2}+5$ has a critical point in the interval $[2,4],$ use the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from ${x}_{0}=3.5$ to estimate the critical point. Round your final answer to two decimal places." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557251 1072 #uuid "4f871b7b-ad2f-55da-b644-daab9cc00d69" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557251 1074 "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({e}^{x}-3{x}^{2}+5)^{′}={e}^{x}-6x, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =({e}^{x}-6x)^{′}={e}^{x}-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-6{x}_{n}}{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}({e}^{{x}_{n}}-6)-({e}^{{x}_{n}}-6{x}_{n})}{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n}-1)}{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=3.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{0}}({x}_{0}-1)}{{e}^{{x}_{0}}-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3.5}(3.5-1)}{{e}^{3.5}-6} \\\\  & =3.053\\,190…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}-1)}{{e}^{{x}_{1}}-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3.053\\,190…}(3.053\\,190…-1)}{{e}^{3.053\\,190…}-6} \\\\  & =2.864\\,577…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{2}}({x}_{2}-1)}{{e}^{{x}_{2}}-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2.864\\,577…}(2.864\\,577…-1)}{{e}^{2.864\\,577…}-6} \\\\  & =2.833\\,890…. \\\\ {x}_{4} & =\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{3}}({x}_{3}-1)}{{e}^{{x}_{3}}-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2.833\\,890…}(2.833\\,890…-1)}{{e}^{2.833\\,890…}-6} \\\\  & =2.833\\,149….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{3}≈2.83$ and ${x}_{4}≈2.83$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the critical point of the function is $x≈2.83,$ rounded to two decimal places." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557252 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557144 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557252 1069 "q-51839" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557252 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557252 1071 "An arithmetic sequence has ${a}_{9}=9$ and ${a}_{22}=178.$ What is the common difference of the sequence?" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557252 1072 #uuid "56794a38-11f6-536d-bb01-6b7e50ccfb8b" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557252 1074 "The first thing to note is that the terms are *increasing*, and therefore we expect $d$ to be *positive*.\n\nWe are given the terms\n\n$$\n{a}_{9}=9,\\,{a}_{22}=178.\n$$\n\nTo find $d,$ we apply the formula with $n=22$ and $m=9:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{n}-{a}_{m}}{n-m} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{22}-{a}_{9}}{22-9} \\\\  & =\\frac{178-9}{22-9} \\\\  & =\\frac{169}{13} \\\\  & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common difference is $d=13.$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557253 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557174 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557253 1069 "q-95392" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557253 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557253 1071 "Consider the change of variables given by the matrix equation\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand the curve $C,$ defined as\n\n$$\nC:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2}=\\frac{119}{8}.\n$$\n\nGiven that the change of variables above reduces $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}$ to its canonical (diagonal) form $2{y}_{1}^{2}-3{y}_{2}^{2},$ what type of a quadratic curve is defined by $C?$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557253 1072 #uuid "584e6ba7-6a66-5994-b1b0-75bfa6a71313" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557253 1074 "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\,{{{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}+2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2}=\\frac{119}{8}\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly: \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{119}{8} \\\\ 2{y}_{1}^{2}-3{y}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{5}(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2})+\\sqrt{5}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2}) & =\\frac{119}{8} \\\\ 2{y}_{1}^{2}-3{y}_{2}^{2}+5{y}_{1} & =\\frac{119}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable ${y}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1}^{2}-3{y}_{2}^{2}+5{y}_{1} & =\\frac{119}{8} \\\\ 2({y}_{1}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}{y}_{1})-3{y}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{119}{8} \\\\ 2({y}_{1}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}{y}_{1}+\\frac{25}{16}-\\frac{25}{16})-3{y}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{119}{8} \\\\ 2{({y}_{1}+\\frac{5}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{25}{8}-3{y}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{119}{8} \\\\ 2{({y}_{1}+\\frac{5}{4})}^{2}-3{y}_{2}^{2} & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1}={y}_{1}+\\frac{5}{4}, \\\\ {z}_{2}={y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{z}_{1}^{2}-3{z}_{2}^{2} & =18 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}-\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{6})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents a hyperbola with semiaxes $3$ and $\\sqrt{6}.$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557254 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557085 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557254 1069 "q-147752" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557254 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557254 1071 "Suppose the following random sample was drawn from the uniform distribution $U[0,1].$ \n\n$$\n0.405,\\,0.824,\\,0.892\n$$\n\nUse these numbers to generate three random numbers that follow a probability distribution with the PDF $f(x),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8}x,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 4, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRound your answers to $3$ decimal places." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557254 1072 #uuid "58d37898-ce2e-573d-a33c-7ebee5e8073b" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557254 1074 "Let the random variable $Y∼U[0,1]$ be uniformly distributed. Suppose that the function $F(x)$ is a cumulative distribution function, where $F(a)=0,F(b)=1,$ and $F$ is strictly monotonic on $a<x<b.$ Then, the random variable $X,$ given by\n\n$$\nX={F}^{-1}(Y)\n$$\n\nis a continuous random variable with the cumulative distribution function $F(x).$\n\nFirst, we must find the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$ corresponding to our random variable. Since our PDF $f(x)$ is non-negative only for $0\\le x\\le 4,$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & \\,x<0, \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & \\,0\\le x\\le 4, \\\\ 1, & \\,x>4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{1}{8}t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}{t}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & \\,x<0, \\\\ \\frac{1}{16}{x}^{2}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 4, \\\\ 1, & \\,x>4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we need to find the inverse of $y=F(x).$ So, we solve the following equation for $x,$ taking into account that $0\\le x\\le 4$ and $0<y<1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{16}{x}^{2} \\\\ 16y & ={x}^{2} \\\\ x & =4\\sqrt{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the numbers drawn from $U[0,1]$ into the above equation:\n\n- For $y=0.405,$ we get $x=4\\sqrt{0.405}≈2.546$\n\n- For $y=0.824,$ we get $x=4\\sqrt{0.824}≈3.631$\n\n- For $y=0.892,$ we get $x=4\\sqrt{0.892}≈3.778$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557255 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557101 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557255 1069 "q-140899" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557255 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557255 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Q$ shown above. Find the matrix $R$ such that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of $A.$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557255 1072 #uuid "6466be13-c99b-575a-b71f-807fcb74c35a" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557255 1074 "Since $Q$ has orthonormal columns, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{T}\\cdot A & ={Q}^{T}\\cdot QR \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & =({Q}^{T}\\,Q)R \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & ={I}_{2}R \\\\ R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that ${Q}^{T}Q={I}_{2}$ since the columns of $Q$ are orthonormal.\n\nNow, we calculate the matrix $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} & -\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557256 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557098 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557256 1069 "q-352256" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557256 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557256 1071 "Consider the finite region bounded by the curve $y=x-3{x}^{2}$ and the $x$-axis. Calculate the volume $V$ of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557256 1072 #uuid "64dd5dee-1a80-5aec-aff5-ee2daf8b8dbf" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557256 1074 "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ x(1-3x) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{1}{3}.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=\\frac{1}{3}.$  \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1/3}x(x-3{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1/3}({x}^{2}-3{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{4})|}_{0}^{1/3} \\\\  & =2\\pi [(\\frac{1}{3\\cdot {3}^{3}}-\\frac{3}{4\\cdot {3}^{4}})-0] \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{1}{324}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{162}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557257 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557116 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557257 1069 "q-115166" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557257 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557257 1071 "![](images/b7/b732a88bc43e0073d7056c536469ac0b959988dd45f385a605ff57432827f334.png)\n\nThe graph above is a translation of $y=\\sqrt{x}.$ Identify the equation that generated the graph." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557257 1072 #uuid "65e9f7af-d6cc-5457-8d42-df8e93d622fa" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557257 1074 "To obtain the given graph, we can perform the following transformations:\n\n- Take the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\n- Translate the graph to the left by $3$ units to get the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x+3}.$\n\n- Translate the result down by $1$ unit to get the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x+3}-1.$\n\nThe result is the solid blue curve shown below.\n\n![](images/b6/b6f0f056c74b60cc8a548ae14d10512c929f378285591996d440da97cb3ed19e.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y=\\sqrt{x+3}-1.$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557258 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557109 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557258 1069 "q-147778" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557258 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557258 1071 "Suppose the following random sample was drawn from the uniform distribution $U[0,1].$ \n\n$$\n0.198,\\,0.266,\\,0.322\n$$\n\nUse these numbers to generate three random numbers that follow a probability distribution with the PDF $f(x),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}},\\, & 1\\le x\\le 4, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRound your answers to $3$ decimal places." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557258 1072 #uuid "67a7dadb-337f-50e0-a564-1d8c6c18e0d9" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557258 1074 "Let the random variable $Y∼U[0,1]$ be uniformly distributed. Suppose that the function $F(x)$ is a cumulative distribution function, where $F(a)=0,F(b)=1,$ and $F$ is strictly monotonic on $a<x<b.$ Then, the random variable $X,$ given by\n\n$$\nX={F}^{-1}(Y)\n$$\n\nis a continuous random variable with the cumulative distribution function $F(x).$\n\nFirst, we must find the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$ corresponding to our random variable. Since our PDF $f(x)$ is non-negative only for $1\\le x\\le 4,$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & \\,x<1, \\\\ {\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & \\,1\\le x\\le 4, \\\\ 1, & \\,x>4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{x}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t}}\\,{|}_{1}^{x} \\\\  & =2(1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & \\,x<1, \\\\ 2(1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}), & \\,1\\le x\\le 4, \\\\ 1, & \\,x>4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we need to find the inverse of $y=F(x).$ So, we solve the following equation for $x,$ taking into account that $1\\le x\\le 4$ and $0<y<1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2(1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}) \\\\ \\frac{y}{2} & =1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}} & =1-\\frac{y}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}} & =\\frac{2-y}{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{x} & =\\frac{2}{2-y} \\\\ x & =\\frac{4}{(2-y)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the numbers drawn from $U[0,1]$ into the above equation:\n\n- For $y=0.198,$ we get $x=\\frac{4}{(2-0.198)^{2}}≈1.232$\n\n- For $y=0.266,$ we get $x=\\frac{4}{(2-0.266)^{2}}≈1.330$\n\n- For $y=0.322,$ we get $x=\\frac{4}{(2-0.322)^{2}}≈1.421$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557259 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557163 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557259 1069 "q-107288" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557259 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557259 1071 "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x+1}$ at the point where $x=4.$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557259 1072 #uuid "67f07fb8-cc06-5cda-9a1f-cc029f68242d" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557259 1074 "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=2x+1.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=4} & ={(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x+1}})|}_{x=4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2(4)+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557260 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557096 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557260 1069 "q-139647" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557260 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557260 1071 "| $x$ | **$-3$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $2$ | $5$ | $7$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above.\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{104}[\\begin{aligned}26 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{X}^{T}y=[\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ 26\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557260 1072 #uuid "685118c5-b7fa-5fb3-b5f9-06f8a56c7a87" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557260 1074 "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{104}[\\begin{aligned}26 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ 26\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3.5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $y=3.5+x.$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557261 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557191 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557261 1069 "q-24089" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557261 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557261 1071 "![](images/66/66d61dadbaed0bb64bebc27286d2b574e63ce71c5c359a2da6b4f3f1a29ee6a9.png)\n\nThe graph above is a translation of $y=\\sqrt{x}.$ Identify the equation that generated the graph." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557261 1072 #uuid "6a7fd6db-b3ef-597a-8e12-d89a63fe1013" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557261 1074 "To obtain the given graph, we can perform the following transformations:\n\n- Take the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\n- Translate the graph to the right by $1$ unit to get the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x-1}.$\n\n- Translate the result up by $2$ units to get the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x-1}+2.$\n\nThe result is the solid blue curve shown below.\n\n![](images/ea/ea0e33e6af15954c3a58b873c82d7877bd3d82595feea8e51b273345309f3241.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y=\\sqrt{x-1}+2.$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557262 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557100 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557262 1069 "q-67962" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557262 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557262 1071 "A certain satellite makes $\\frac{1}{3}$  of its orbit around the Earth in $\\frac{1}{6}$ of a day. How many times can it orbit Earth in $1$ day?" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557262 1072 #uuid "6aea863a-357f-52de-8ad7-b9c9c32993ae" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557262 1074 "To find how many  times the satellite  can go around the Earth in $1$ day, we can compute the orbital unit rate per day. We do this by dividing the amount of the orbit the satellite travels, $\\frac{1}{3},$ by the corresponding fraction of a day, $\\frac{1}{6},$ as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}÷\\frac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}÷\\frac{1}{6} & =\\frac{1}{3}\\times \\frac{6}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\times 6}{3\\times 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the satellite can orbit Earth $2$ times in $1$ day." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557263 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557140 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557263 1069 "q-67967" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557263 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557263 1071 "Riding his electric bike, Matt can go $\\frac{1}{2}$ of a mile in $\\frac{3}{4}$  minutes. What is the unit rate of miles covered per minute?" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557263 1072 #uuid "6cccdce8-916f-545e-8523-5e2c1794424a" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557263 1074 "To compute the unit rate of miles per minute, we divide the number of miles, $\\frac{1}{2},$ by the number of minutes, $\\frac{3}{4}$, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}÷\\frac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}÷\\frac{3}{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\times \\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\times 4}{2\\times 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\times 2}{2\\times 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{2}\\times 2}{\\cancel{2}\\times 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the unit rate is $\\frac{2}{3}$ miles per minute." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557264 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557180 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557264 1069 "q-76082" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557264 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557264 1071 "What is the set of values of $x$ that satisfy $6\\le 3x+9\\le 15$ or $4x-3\\ge 5?$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557264 1072 #uuid "6d993076-511f-5d5a-b4e5-bf19ec4e8490" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557264 1074 "We start by solving $6\\le 3x+9\\le 15:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 & \\le 3x+9\\le 15 \\\\ -3 & \\le 3x\\le 6 \\\\ -1 & \\le x\\le 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/ab/ab1b1f6494c00b463e7912ab3f9710aee553e3a99484822e678494b55268e398.png)\n\nThen, we solve $4x-3\\ge 5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-3 & \\ge 5 \\\\ 4x & \\ge 8 \\\\ x & \\ge 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/1b/1b0700462c3957759abbe608c300fece27f92dfdb9b8c14dbeb44cc053de7784.png)\n\nTo graph the values of $x$ that satisfy either inequality, we overlay the two graphs on the same number line:\n\n![](images/25/25f4136a1e248ab30fc0c01feef1e27c331553c1adcf4a1c27c133764cca1ae1.png)\n\nTherefore, the final solution is $x\\ge -1.$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557265 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557115 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557265 1069 "q-112131" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557265 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557265 1071 "Which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=(x-3)(x-1)^{2}?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557265 1072 #uuid "6ec517e9-4418-5776-9805-f9f4171a3217" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557265 1074 "The equation is already in factored form, so by the zero-product rule, we deduce that it has a single root at $x=3$ and a double root at $x=1.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(0-3)(0-1)^{2} \\\\  & =(-3)(-1)^{2} \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,-3),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=3,$ and\n\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=1.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/ba/baff75d3cb8119a39819b82cca053f686ce33ebfc86f963422e1a0653a9f842b.png)" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557266 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557205 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557266 1069 "q-139201" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557266 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557266 1071 "![](images/78/780120c106065874c04d97b8b6ef10e3e81e1cf9fd7c7b19bc633963cbadafe4.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $y=4x-2{x}^{2}$ and $y=2{x}^{3},$ as shown above. Find an integral expression for the volume generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557266 1072 #uuid "6fc2e8c7-fc0f-5be7-9a14-96326d528b23" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557266 1074 "First, let $f(x)=4x-2{x}^{2}$ and $g(x)=2{x}^{3}.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ 4x-2{x}^{2} & =2{x}^{3} \\\\ 4x-2{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ -2x(-2+x+{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ x(x-1)(x+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=-2,$ $x=0,$  and $x=1.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=1$ because we are only concerned about $x\\ge 0.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual vertical arrow through the region to determine the lower and upper functions.\n\n![](images/e6/e695cfd83116df98b1530e2d53186de0ebcd03c6056c2b8d7c2272a76fbd790c.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=g(x)=2{x}^{3},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=f(x)=4x-2{x}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x([\\text{upper}]-[\\text{lower}])\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}x([4x-2{x}^{2}]-[2{x}^{3}])\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}x(4x-2{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557267 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557131 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557267 1069 "q-67874" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557267 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557267 1071 "A race car covers $\\frac{4}{5}$ of a lap in  $2$ minutes.  What is the unit rate of laps covered per minute?" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557267 1072 #uuid "70693bce-1563-5043-b145-f1732cc4b8df" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557267 1074 "To compute the unit rate of laps per minute, we divide the number of laps, $\\frac{4}{5},$ by the number of seconds, $2$, as follows:     \n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{5}÷2\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by a whole number is equivalent to multiplying the fraction by the reciprocal of that whole number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5}÷2 & =\\frac{4}{5}÷\\frac{2}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\times \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\times 1}{5\\times 2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\times 2}{2\\times 5} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{2}\\times 2}{\\cancel{2}\\times 5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the unit rate in this situation is $\\frac{2}{5}$ laps per minute." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557268 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557157 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557268 1069 "q-67840" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557268 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557268 1071 "Johnny walked $\\frac{3}{8}$ of a mile in  $7$ minutes, keeping an even pace. How many miles did he walk in $1$ minute?" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557268 1072 #uuid "71f0d135-eabe-57fb-ab96-cf20fb60fc0a" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557268 1074 "To compute the number of miles Johnny  walked in $1$ minute, we can compute the unit rate of miles per minute. We do this by dividing the number of miles, $\\frac{3}{8},$ by the number of minute, as follows:   \n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{8}÷7\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by a whole number is equivalent to multiplying the fraction by the reciprocal of that whole number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8}÷7 & =\\frac{3}{8}\\times \\frac{1}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\times 1}{8\\times 7} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{56}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, Johnny walked  $\\frac{3}{56}$ miles in $1$ minute." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557269 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557112 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557269 1069 "q-25057" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557269 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557269 1071 "In the right triangle $GHI,$ $∠G$ is a right angle, $GH=24,$ $HI=26$ and $GI=10.$ What is the value of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡I?$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557269 1072 #uuid "76554d51-a4eb-517f-a2a7-3724b0e31c74" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557269 1074 "Let's draw the triangle.\n\n![](images/14/1429b33d88f438268a4168f57ff1417586c74e2dd034ff6241e0d2fddb6146b2.png)\n\nWe compute the cosecant of the angle $I$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡I=\\frac{\\text{hypotenuse}}{\\text{opposite}}=\\frac{26}{24}=\\frac{13}{12}\n$$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557270 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557206 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557270 1069 "q-143273" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557270 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557270 1071 "Given that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{49}∼N(-7,{14}^{2})$ is a random sample of size $n=49$ from a normal population, what is the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}?$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557270 1072 #uuid "76dee0de-583e-58f7-b45a-1c6693fa7da9" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557270 1074 "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a normal population, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ has the distribution\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nn=49,\\,\\mu =-7,\\,\\sigma =14.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(-7,\\frac{{14}^{2}}{49}) \\\\  & ∼N(-7,4).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557271 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557159 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557271 1069 "q-53580" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557271 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557271 1071 "![](images/7f/7f5f82876a00c72d7ec829f320d021613d1932b85ef87c7981f9f92669792cb9.png)\n\nJessica's circular swimming pool has a radius of $10\\,\\text{ft}.$ What is the area of the pool?" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557271 1072 #uuid "7769fe1f-a9ea-5777-8254-93dfa6c193aa" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557271 1074 "The formula for the area of a circle is $A=\\pi {r}^{2},$ so the area of Jessica's pool is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot {10}^{2} \\\\  & =100\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557272 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557108 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557272 1069 "q-38384" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557272 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557272 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of $y=\\sqrt{-x}-1?$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557272 1072 #uuid "78215167-343c-5511-b0e2-4a5f5a4ce03b" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557272 1074 "To obtain the graph of $y=\\sqrt{-x}-1,$ we can perform the following transformations:\n\n- Take the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\n- Reflect the graph in the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=\\sqrt{-x}.$\n\nThe result is the solid blue curve shown below.\n\n![](images/d0/d078ff010c5bb8a05bfe08bd6585ebb334bda324c69f7c8498bde38dbd8cd234.png)\n\n- Translate the result down by $1$ unit to get the graph of $y=\\sqrt{-x}-1.$\n\nThe result is the solid blue curve shown below.\n\n![](images/28/2832645bc8cac13094899294dcd0cb45b24fb0da8339d5bc9e1e2c055aaee2b8.png)" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557273 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557080 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557273 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557089 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557273 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557103 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557273 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557120 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557273 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557151 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557273 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557170 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557273 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557185 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557273 1069 "q-335009" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557273 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557273 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-2x+3xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=4y-5xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the predators and $y=y(t)$ represents the prey. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ Evaluating the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\,0]^{T},$ we get\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$J({x}^{∗})=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,.$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are \n{{field-5}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}$ is \n{{field-6}}{{field-7}}." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557273 1072 #uuid "79cf6220-8640-5993-92c2-8195fa289578" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557273 1074 "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=-2x+3xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=4y-5xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2x+3xy)=-2+3y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2x+3xy)=3x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4y-5xy)=-5y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4y-5xy)=4-5x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}-2+3y & 3x \\\\ -5y & 4-5x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}-2+3(0) & 3(0) \\\\ -5(0) & 4-5(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=4.$ Since eigenvalues are real with opposite signs, our equilibrium is $\\text{an unstable}$ $\\text{saddle}.$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557274 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557194 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557274 1069 "q-73407" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557274 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557274 1071 "Given that a geometric sequence has \n${a}_{1}=896$, ${a}_{4}=112$ and ${a}_{k}=14$, what is the value of $k?$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557274 1072 #uuid "7b028485-bf8e-559e-a7ed-a6b72baad9fb" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557274 1074 "First, we need to find the common ratio. We are told that ${a}_{1}=896$ and ${a}_{4}=112,$ so we can find the common ratio using a formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{n-m} & =\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}} \\\\ {r}^{4-1} & =\\frac{{a}_{4}}{{a}_{1}} \\\\ {r}^{4-1} & =\\frac{112}{896} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ {r}^{3} & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the common ratio is $r=\\frac{1}{2}$, we can use the same formula to solve for the value of $k$ such that ${a}_{k}=14:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{n-m} & =\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}} \\\\ {r}^{k-4} & =\\frac{{a}_{k}}{{a}_{4}} \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{k-4} & =\\frac{14}{112} \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{k-4} & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{k-4} & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3} \\\\ k-4 & =3 \\\\ k & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557275 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557095 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557275 1069 "q-139576" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557275 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557275 1071 "| $x$ | **$-15$** | **$-10$** | **$-5$** | **$0$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-6$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients  of the line $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above can be found using linear regression as \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557275 1072 #uuid "7fc30a95-4823-5308-bc3d-c38eeb6c9b5d" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557275 1074 "| $x$ | **$-15$** | **$-10$** | **$-5$** | **$0$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-6$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -15 \\\\ 1 & -10 \\\\ 1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557276 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557165 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557276 1069 "q-115388" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557276 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557276 1071 "Find the root of $f(x)=2\\sqrt{5x+10}+3.$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557276 1072 #uuid "821416d3-f1f2-5d6c-b79b-722f962867ca" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557276 1074 "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt{5x+10}+3=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{5x+10}+3 & =0 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{5x+10} & =-3 \\\\ \\sqrt{5x+10} & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any solutions since its left-hand side is always nonnegative, while the right-hand side is negative.\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has no roots." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557277 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557161 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557277 1069 "q-63862" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557277 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557277 1071 "Given that a geometric sequence has the first term ${a}_{1}=5$, the common ratio $r=2$, and the $n$th term ${a}_{n}=160$, what is the value of $n?$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557277 1072 #uuid "88a280e7-5b7e-5d06-845d-b450e3c6973c" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557277 1074 "To find the index, we substitute our known values of ${a}_{1}$, $r$ and ${a}_{n}$ into the formula for the $n$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ 160 & =5\\cdot {2}^{n-1} \\\\ \\frac{160}{5} & ={2}^{n-1} \\\\ 32 & ={2}^{n-1} \\\\ {2}^{5} & ={2}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the exponents gives:\n\n$$\n5=n-1\\,⟹\\,n=6\n$$\n\nWe can check this result by substituting $n=6$ into the formula for the $n$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =5\\cdot {2}^{6-1} \\\\  & =5\\cdot {2}^{5} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 32 \\\\  & =160\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557278 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557169 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557278 1069 "q-45713" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557278 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557278 1071 "The population $C(t)$ of condors in the Andes is given by $C(t)=1\\,000\\,000\\cdot (0.25)^{t}$, where $t$ is the time, in decades, since the last census. How many condors vanished between the second and third decades since the last census?" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557278 1072 #uuid "8c286c29-c804-5f9e-952f-d64fbcc60ae3" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557278 1074 "We need to find the difference in the population of condors between $t=2$ and $t=3$ decades. To calculate this, we find the population after $2$ decades, and we subtract the population after $3$ decades. So we need to evaluate\n\n$$\nC(2)-C(3).\n$$\n\nThe number of condors after $t=2$ decades is equal to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(2) & =1\\,000\\,000\\cdot (0.25)^{2} \\\\  & =1\\,000\\,000\\cdot 0.062\\,5 \\\\  & =62\\,500.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe number of condors after $t=3$ decades is equal to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(3) & =1\\,000\\,000\\cdot (0.25)^{3} \\\\  & =1\\,000\\,000\\cdot 0.015\\,625 \\\\  & =15\\,625.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the number of condors lost between the second and third decades is\n\n$$\nC(3)-C(2)=62\\,500-15\\,625=46\\,875.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $46\\,875$ condors vanished between the second and third decades." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557279 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557181 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557279 1069 "q-67843" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557279 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557279 1071 "A plane can travel $\\frac{3}{5}$ of a mile in $6$ seconds.  What is the unit rate of miles traveled per second?" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557279 1072 #uuid "8c31a83a-d0fb-50d8-8958-8a93c295b8d7" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557279 1074 "To compute the unit rate of miles per second, we divide the number of miles, $\\frac{3}{5},$ by the number of seconds, $6$, as follows:    \n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{5}÷6\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by a whole number is equivalent to multiplying the fraction by the reciprocal of that whole number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5}÷6 & =\\frac{3}{5}÷\\frac{6}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\times \\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\times 1}{5\\times 6} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\times 1}{5\\times 2\\times 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{3}\\times 1}{5\\times 2\\times \\cancel{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the unit rate in this situation is $\\frac{1}{10}$ miles per second." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557280 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557137 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557280 1069 "q-75165" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557280 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557280 1071 "Given that $x-4$ is a factor of $f(x)=-{x}^{3}+10{x}^{2}-32x+32$, which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n*(Note: Figures not drawn to scale)*" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557280 1072 #uuid "8cca0427-a573-5822-8e99-bd72fe2c51d9" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557280 1074 "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x-4)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x-4)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-4)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ 4 & -1 & 10 & -32 & 32 \\\\  &  & -4 & 24 & -32 \\\\  & -1 & 6 & -8 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{f(x)}{x-4} & =-{x}^{2}+6x-8 \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}-6x+8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nf(x)=-(x-4)({x}^{2}-6x+8).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\nf(x)=-(x-4)(x-2)(x-4)=-(x-2)(x-4)^{2}\n$$\n\nNow that we have the cubic in factored form, we can tell that it has a single root at $x=2$ and a double root at $x=4.$\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-(0-2)(0-4)^{2} \\\\  & =-(-2)(-4)^{2} \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,32),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=2,$ and\n\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=4.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph takes an $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/aa/aae66bc5dcf29e5e85e33942541b510d136b4f7f13d633f28c46d4b5a9ca977b.png)" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557281 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557105 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557281 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557147 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557281 1069 "q-334923" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557281 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557281 1071 "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}],\\,x(1)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"180px\">$2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1))+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1))$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td><td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,+{\\int }_{1}^{t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"180px\">${e}^{6s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s))$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,\\text{d}s$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td><td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557281 1072 #uuid "8e6e37b0-420c-5604-b7d4-9e71462bd5eb" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557281 1074 "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)={{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+{{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{⏟}}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}].$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-1)}\\,x(1) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1))+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1)) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1))+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1))\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-s))\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-s))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{6s}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{6s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s)) \\\\ {e}^{6s}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-s))\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1))+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1)) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1))+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1))\\end{aligned}]+{\\int }_{1}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{6s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s)) \\\\ {e}^{6s}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-s))\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557282 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557079 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557282 1069 "q-95925" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557282 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557282 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Q$ shown above. Find the matrix $R$ such that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of $A.$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557282 1072 #uuid "91f32261-8a78-5d95-90b2-1f1ea2788664" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557282 1074 "Since $Q$ has orthonormal columns, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{T}\\cdot A & ={Q}^{T}\\cdot QR \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & =({Q}^{T}\\,Q)R \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & ={I}_{2}R \\\\ R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that ${Q}^{T}Q={I}_{2}$ since the columns of $Q$ are orthonormal.\n\nNow, we calculate the matrix $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557283 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557150 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557283 1069 "q-32723" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557283 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557283 1071 "Given that\n$M=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $M=LU,$ then $L=$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557283 1072 #uuid "9735b81a-0c65-5842-9564-1b88b92db55e" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557283 1074 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $M$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $M$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{5}){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot M \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot M \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}M=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}M & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}M & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ M & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ M & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ M & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ M & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}5 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557284 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557166 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557284 1069 "q-25071" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557284 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557284 1071 "![](images/18/184605ecccca3547b5535ef5783f93fd5e9a65e3367b601eb14fa84962da2972.png)\n\n$\\operatorname{csc}⁡C=$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557284 1072 #uuid "9776a674-b453-514c-8110-e6416b1678fc" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557284 1074 "We compute the cosecant of the angle $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡C=\\frac{\\text{hypotenuse}}{\\text{opposite}}=\\frac{101}{20}.\n$$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557285 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557075 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557285 1069 "q-79020" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557285 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557285 1071 "![](images/22/22f63e5e602b29317d7df70f7dae7387feccdd9ab5678592e6c1af35d3d45fd3.png)\n\nWhat is the area of a circle that has a diameter of $8\\,\\text{m}?$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557285 1072 #uuid "9abf643c-c154-51d5-82bc-efb0d0062ad6" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557285 1074 "The radius of a circle is half the diameter. So, the radius of our circle is $r=4.$\n\nTherefore, the area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot {4}^{2} \\\\  & =16\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557286 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557149 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557286 1069 "q-256827" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557286 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557286 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4{x}^{2}$ in the interval $[-2,2].$ Calculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from the point ${x}_{0}=2.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557286 1072 #uuid "9ad7acb8-7337-5a52-9975-3f2fd0fd9208" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557286 1074 "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4{x}^{2})^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-8x, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-8x)^{′}=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}_{n})+{x}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{n})-8{x}_{n}}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{n})-{x}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}_{n})-8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}_{0})+{x}_{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{0})-8{x}_{0}}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{0})-{x}_{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}_{0})-8} \\\\  & =2-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2)-8(2)}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2)-8} \\\\  & =0.505\\,007…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}_{1})+{x}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{1})-8{x}_{1}}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{1})-{x}_{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}_{1})-8} \\\\  & =(0.505\\,007…)-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.505\\,007…)+(0.505\\,007…)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0.505\\,007…)-8(0.505\\,007…)}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0.505\\,007…)-(0.505\\,007…)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.505\\,007…)-8} \\\\  & ≈0.025,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557287 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557122 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557287 1069 "q-143282" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557287 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557287 1071 "Given that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{24}∼N(-5,{12}^{2})$ is a random sample of size $24$ from a normal population, calculate $P(\\overset{―}{X}<-4.6).$\n\n*Hint: A value table of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.0\\,$ | $\\,0.5160\\,$ | $\\,0.5199\\,$ | $\\,0.5239\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.5557\\,$ | $\\,0.5596\\,$ | $\\,0.5636\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.5948\\,$ | $\\,0.5987\\,$ | $\\,0.6026\\,$ |" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557287 1072 #uuid "9af82014-8871-57e9-a191-d8c7d4765bcf" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557287 1074 "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a normal population, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ has the distribution\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(-5,\\frac{{12}^{2}}{24}) \\\\  & ∼N(-5,6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're required to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}<-4.6).$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<-4.6) & =P(Z<\\frac{-4.6-(-5)}{\\sqrt{6}}) \\\\  & =P(Z<0.16) \\\\  & =Φ(0.16)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.16)=0.5636.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<-4.6) & =Φ(0.16) \\\\  & =0.5636.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557288 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557199 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557288 1069 "q-115372" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557288 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557288 1071 "What is the domain of $f(x)=5\\sqrt{4x-3}?$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557288 1072 #uuid "9e99c86a-1348-545b-9c9f-3a1428e5141e" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557288 1074 "The domain of $y=\\sqrt{x}$ is\n\n$$\nx\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the domain of $f(x)=5\\sqrt{4x-3},$ we replace $x$ with $4x-3$ in the above inequality. This gives\n\n$$\n4x-3\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-3 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 4x & \\ge 3 \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $y=f(x)$ is $x\\in [\\frac{3}{4},\\infty ).$\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/f7/f71e9e717ab5c217d91528de89540de9013eadd31b1907c89d43d589fb6929af.png)" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557289 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557078 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557289 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557114 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557289 1069 "q-334828" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557289 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557289 1071 "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}],\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">$(t-s){e}^{4t}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,\\text{d}s$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">${e}^{4t}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557289 1072 #uuid "9f8ace62-6215-5f44-93f8-e30dfc0372e6" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557289 1074 "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)={{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+{{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{⏟}}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}].$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At}\\,x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{4(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4(t-s)}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{4(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4(t-s)}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{4s}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(t-s){e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]+{\\int }_{0}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}(t-s){e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557290 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557081 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557290 1069 "q-162579" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557290 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557290 1071 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=2,$ $y=1,$ $y=3,$ $z=1,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=x\\sqrt{y}\\,i-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,j+xy\\,k,$ the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557290 1072 #uuid "a809785e-b48f-5cb0-92f3-ff24733e01ab" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557290 1074 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\sqrt{y})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{y}-\\frac{1}{y}+0 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{y}-\\frac{1}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}(\\sqrt{y}-\\frac{1}{y})\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\sqrt{y}-\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557291 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557124 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557291 1069 "q-102023" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557291 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557291 1071 "What is the index of the term $2.56$ for the geometric sequence below?\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=0.4{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=16,\\,n\\ge 1\n$$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557291 1072 #uuid "aae39f44-86a0-57f2-8ef2-2ffdbe1ad780" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557291 1074 "This geometric sequence is given as a recurrence relation, i.e.,\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=r\\cdot {a}_{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=16,\\,r=0.4.\n$$\n\nWe're interested in finding $n$ such that ${a}_{n}=2.56.$ Using the formula for the $n$th term, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ 2.56 & =16\\cdot {0.4}^{n-1} \\\\ \\frac{2.56}{16} & ={0.4}^{n-1} \\\\ 0.16 & ={0.4}^{n-1} \\\\ {0.4}^{2} & ={0.4}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the exponents gives:\n\n$$\n2=n-1\\,⟹\\,n=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, $2.56$ is the $3$rd term of the sequence." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557292 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557152 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557292 1069 "q-115377" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557292 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557292 1071 "What is the domain of $f(x)=3\\sqrt{8x}-2?$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557292 1072 #uuid "acd7fcaf-37e0-5893-ba70-06ebe65a32f1" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557292 1074 "The domain of $y=\\sqrt{x}$ is\n\n$$\nx\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the domain of $f(x)=3\\sqrt{8x}-2,$ we replace $x$ with $8x$ in the above inequality. This gives\n\n$$\n8x\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x & \\ge 0 \\\\ x & \\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $y=f(x)$ is $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/ef/efbc925cd1aca7f476d6cfc11f3c668c4f3bde78acf359eb0a65696ce2a12852.png)" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557293 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557175 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557293 1069 "q-161105" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557293 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557293 1071 "![](images/0b/0b460a07026e489fb7c63dad99c04c7e44540138632626dcd3ebcafb5c0a93d3.png)\n\nThe area of $ABCD$ is $52{\\text{ in}}^{2}.$   What is the area of $△BCD$ rounded to the nearest integer?" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557293 1072 #uuid "b105592d-99b2-5bf4-877e-0bc7baf37cd7" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557293 1074 "Let's start by finding the area of $△ABD.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot AB\\cdot AD\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 9\\cdot 16\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{66}^{∘} \\\\  & =72\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{66}^{∘} \\\\  & =65.775… \\\\  & ≈66{\\text{ in}}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nThe difference of the areas of  $△ABD$ and the quadrilateral $ABCD$ gives the area of $△BCD.$ Therefore, \n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△BCD} & ={A}_{△ABD}-{A}_{ABCD} \\\\  & =66-52 \\\\  & =14\\,{\\text{ in}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557294 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557177 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557294 1069 "q-139575" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557294 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557294 1071 "| $x$ | **$-1$** | **$1$** | **$3$** | **$5$** | **$7$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $4$ | $8$ | $11$ | $14$ | $20$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients  of the line $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above can be found using linear regression as \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557294 1072 #uuid "b1f3a877-5233-5d11-bd7f-f8b8db99c5be" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557294 1074 "| $x$ | **$-1$** | **$1$** | **$3$** | **$5$** | **$7$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $4$ | $8$ | $11$ | $14$ | $20$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 11 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557295 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557110 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557295 1069 "q-147776" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557295 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557295 1071 "Suppose the following random sample was drawn from the uniform distribution $U[0,1].$ \n\n$$\n0.041,\\,0.137,\\,0.782\n$$\n\nUse these numbers to generate three random numbers that follow a probability distribution with the PDF $f(x),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}},\\, & x\\ge 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRound your answers to $3$ decimal places." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557295 1072 #uuid "b352c39a-e73c-5f2e-8ba3-a028566bd47b" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557295 1074 "Let the random variable $Y∼U[0,1]$ be uniformly distributed. Suppose that the function $F(x)$ is a cumulative distribution function, where $F(a)=0,F(b)=1,$ and $F$ is strictly monotonic on $a<x<b.$ Then, the random variable $X,$ given by\n\n$$\nX={F}^{-1}(Y)\n$$\n\nis a continuous random variable with the cumulative distribution function $F(x).$\n\nFirst, we must find the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$ corresponding to our random variable. Since our PDF $f(x)$ is non-negative only for $x\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & \\,x<1, \\\\ {\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & \\,x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{x}\\frac{2}{{t}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,{|}_{1}^{x} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & \\,x<1, \\\\ 1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}, & \\,x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we need to find the inverse of $y=F(x).$ So, we solve the following equation for $x,$ taking into account that $x\\ge 1$ and $0<y<1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & =1-y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{1-y} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the numbers drawn from $U[0,1]$ into the above equation:\n\n- For $y=0.041,$ we get $x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-0.041}}≈1.021$\n\n- For $y=0.137,$ we get $x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-0.137}}≈1.076$\n\n- For $y=0.782,$ we get $x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-0.782}}≈2.142$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557296 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557097 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557296 1069 "q-71431" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557296 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557296 1071 "An air compressor moves $\\frac{8}{15}$ gallons of air in four seconds. Find the unit rate of gallons per second." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557296 1072 #uuid "bde894c0-0f60-52a1-8f53-44362f2c04ac" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557296 1074 "To compute the unit rate of gallons per second, we divide the number of gallons, $\\frac{8}{15},$ by the number of seconds, $4$, as follows:   \n\n$$\n\\frac{8}{15}÷4\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by a whole number is equivalent to multiplying the fraction by the reciprocal of that whole number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{15}÷4 & =\\frac{8}{15}÷\\frac{4}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{15}\\times \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\times 1}{15\\times 4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\times 4}{15\\times 4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\times \\cancel{4}}{15\\times \\cancel{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit rate in this situation is $\\frac{2}{15}$ gallons per second." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557297 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557146 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557297 1069 "q-277380" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557297 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557297 1071 "The root of the function $f(x)=-\\sqrt{8-2x}+4$ is $x=${{field-1}}." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557297 1072 #uuid "be201399-7461-56d1-865b-23d766571416" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557297 1074 "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n-\\sqrt{8-2x}+4=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{8-2x}+4 & =0 \\\\ -\\sqrt{8-2x} & =-4 \\\\ \\sqrt{8-2x} & =4 \\\\ (\\sqrt{8-2x})^{2} & ={4}^{2} \\\\ 8-2x & =16 \\\\ -2x & =8 \\\\ x & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we must check the answer by substituting it back into the initial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{8-2(-4)}+4 & =0 \\\\ -\\sqrt{8+8}+4 & =0 \\\\ -\\sqrt{16}+4 & =0 \\\\ -4+4 & =0 \\\\ -4+4 & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=-4.$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557298 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557196 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557298 1069 "q-147598" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557298 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557298 1071 "Suppose the following random sample was drawn from the uniform distribution $U[0,1].$ \n\n$$\n0.112,\\,0.455,\\,0.800\n$$\n\nUse these numbers to generate three random numbers that follow a probability distribution with the CDF $F(x),$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}, & \\,x\\le 0, \\\\ 1, & \\,x>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRound your answers to $3$ decimal places." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557298 1072 #uuid "be496a82-fb90-5b74-9be3-d9e0bf10ddb1" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557298 1074 "Let the random variable $Y∼U[0,1]$ be uniformly distributed. Suppose that the function $F(x)$ is a cumulative distribution function, where $F(a)=0,F(b)=1,$ and $F$ is strictly monotonic on $a<x<b.$ Then, the random variable $X,$ given by\n\n$$\nX={F}^{-1}(Y)\n$$\n\nis a continuous random variable with the cumulative distribution function $F(x).$\n\nSo, we need to find the inverse of $y=F(x).$ To do this, we solve the following equation for $x,$ taking into account that $x\\le 0$ and $0<y<1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{x} \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the numbers drawn from $U[0,1]$ into the above equation:\n\n- For $y=0.112,$ we get $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.112)≈-2.189$\n\n- For $y=0.455,$ we get $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.455)≈-0.787$\n\n- For $y=0.800,$ we get $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.800)≈-0.223$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557299 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557139 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557299 1069 "q-115161" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557299 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557299 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x+3}+4?$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557299 1072 #uuid "c029c003-43fa-5232-9220-57dfcb421802" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557299 1074 "To obtain the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x+3}+4,$ we can perform the following transformations:\n\n- Take the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\n- Translate the graph to the left by $3$ units to get the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x+3}.$\n\n- Translate the result up by $4$ units to get the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x+3}+4.$\n\nThe result is the solid blue curve shown below.\n\n![](images/07/07838d780998cbf654b02601c649b2548c8421533d1e1bb9ef982d025632d965.png)" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557300 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557198 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557300 1069 "q-140553" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557300 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557300 1071 "Which of the following equations defines two intersecting lines in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane?" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557300 1072 #uuid "c0f59d2f-ea36-559d-85fe-96af7c52c659" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557300 1074 "The correct answer is \n\n$$\n{C}_{3}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{3}-{x}_{2}^{2}=0.\n$$\n\nThis equation defines two intersecting lines, ${x}_{1}=\\sqrt{3}{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{1}=-\\sqrt{3}{x}_{2},$ in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane.\n\nNotice that the other equations do not define two parallel lines:\n\n- ${C}_{1}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{3}-{x}_{2}^{2}=1$ is the equation of a hyperbola with semiaxes $\\sqrt{3}$ and $1.$\n\n- ${C}_{2}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{3}+{x}_{2}^{2}=0$ defines a single point $(0,0)$ in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane.\n\n- ${C}_{4}\\,:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}=3$ defines two parallel lines, ${x}_{1}=\\sqrt{3}$ and ${x}_{1}=-\\sqrt{3},$ in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane.\n\n- ${C}_{5}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{3}+{x}_{2}^{2}=1$ is the equation of an ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt{3}$ and $1.$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557301 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557088 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557301 1069 "q-314205" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557301 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557301 1071 "Given that $f(x)={(6x-5)}^{6},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557301 1072 #uuid "c1385fb4-0635-5e4c-a92d-2175e82b7bcc" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557301 1074 "Let $g(x)=6x-5$ and $f(g(x))=g(x)^{6}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{6})^{′}=6{g}^{5}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(6x-5)^{′}=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =6{g}^{5}\\cdot (6) \\\\  & =36(6x-5)^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557302 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557086 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557302 1069 "q-139276" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557302 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557302 1071 "![](images/a9/a9db1a85c150c0bd6a8913a19387c7c717103cff1faf5266c7e21e774ab193ab.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $y=-{x}^{2}+2x+1$ and $y={x}^{2}+1,$ as shown above. Find an integral expression for the volume generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557302 1072 #uuid "c1c69fb5-f0aa-5c39-a555-57a6bac7db2e" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557302 1074 "First, let $f(x)=-{x}^{2}+2x+1$ and $g(x)={x}^{2}+1.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ -{x}^{2}+2x+1 & ={x}^{2}+1 \\\\ -2{x}^{2}+2x & =0 \\\\ -2x(x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=0,$ and $x=1.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=1.$  \n\nNow, let's draw our usual vertical arrow through the region to determine the lower and upper functions.\n\n![](images/87/8790ae667c9a2741e2a732ff69d9afe5633dc26df7b02eee52fb51478524282b.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=g(x)={x}^{2}+1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=f(x)=-{x}^{2}+2x+1.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x([\\text{upper}]-[\\text{lower}])\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}x([-{x}^{2}+2x+1]-[{x}^{2}+1])\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}x(2x-2{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557303 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557195 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557303 1069 "q-37888" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557303 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557303 1071 "![](images/ef/ef42744182deb0d83704b13fba2324b92df197151c3566de01bb75a202e836cc.png)\n\nWhat are the roots of the function $f(x)$ defined by the mapping diagram shown above?" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557303 1072 #uuid "c306bf08-706c-5a8c-b907-b249d9ec68eb" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557303 1074 "The roots of the function are the values of $x$ such that $f(x)=0.$ In the given mapping diagram, we see that $f(\\frac{1}{3})=0.$\n\nTherefore, the only root of the function is $x=\\frac{1}{3}.$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557304 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557133 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557304 1069 "q-89992" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557304 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557304 1071 "Find the root of $f(x)=\\sqrt{4-5x}+10.$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557304 1072 #uuid "c6dd5656-22b2-5e5b-b391-f41ec70a470b" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557304 1074 "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{4-5x}+10=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{4-5x}+10 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{4-5x} & =-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any solutions since its left-hand side is always nonnegative, while the right-hand side is negative.\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has no roots." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557305 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557069 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557305 1069 "q-67785" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557305 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557305 1071 "![](images/27/27dc9ff8452d9a9aa95322492549ba0eabed5f68a7aa2e586ebe239143268bde.png)\n\nRounded to the nearest integer, what is the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD?$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557305 1072 #uuid "c86bcf22-54c7-51dc-bd7b-9dc7538670ba" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557305 1074 "The area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is the sum of the areas of the triangles $△ABD$ and $△BDC$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/da/da7cc00005fbeb686443e2075009fda7c68f6d379a8ddb924a7dfe5b60e6ab16.png)\n\n \n\nFirst we find the area for $△ABD:$ \n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 10\\cdot 8\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{75}^{∘} \\\\  & =40\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{75}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈38.637,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nNow we find the area for $△BCD:$ \n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{BDC} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot 12\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{67.2}^{∘} \\\\  & =30\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{67.2}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈27.656,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nFinally, we add together the areas of our two triangles to determine the area of $ABCD:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABCD} & ={A}_{ABD}+{A}_{BDC} \\\\  & =38.637+27.656 \\\\  & =66.293\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, to the nearest integer, the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is $66.$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557306 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557207 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557306 1069 "q-67905" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557306 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557306 1071 "A leaking paddling pool loses $6$ liters of water in  $\\frac{2}{3}$ hours. How much water will it lose in $1$ hour?" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557306 1072 #uuid "c922cf86-366d-5b96-b6c8-e7d64dc90ff6" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557306 1074 "To find out how much water the pool will lose in $1$ hour, we can compute the unit rate of liters of water lost per hour. We do this by dividing  the number of liters, $6,$ by the number of hours, $\\frac{2}{3}$, as follows:     \n\n$$\n6÷\\frac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nTo divide by a fraction, we multiply by the fraction's reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6÷\\frac{2}{3} & =6\\times \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{1}\\times \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\times 3}{1\\times 2} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{2} \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the pool will lose $9$ liters of water in $1$ hour." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557307 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557156 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557307 1069 "q-343986" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557307 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557307 1071 "$$\n{y}^{″}+6y=f(x),\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=5\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above, where $f(x)$ is an odd, $4$-periodic function. Select the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the second infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}x)\n$$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557307 1072 #uuid "c9b0aad8-60f7-5ec4-9cbf-7d3e0f654c83" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557307 1074 "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(x)$ is given by $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},$ where ${y}_{c}$ is the solution to the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we address the particular solution ${y}_{p}.$ Since $f(x)$ is an odd function, its Fourier series consists only of sine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an odd function. This is represented by the term\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}x).\n$$\n\nNext, we find the homogeneous solution. The characteristic equation ${\\lambda }^{2}+6=0$ gives roots $\\lambda =\\pm \\sqrt{6}\\text{i}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{6}x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{6}x).\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we apply the initial conditions. Using $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ 0 & =A(1)+0+0 \\\\ A & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ because the particular solution is a sine series.\n\nNext, we use the derivative condition ${y}^{′}(0)=5.$ First, we compute the derivative of the total solution (with $A=0$):\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)∼B\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{6}x)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n\\pi }{2}{b}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}x)\n$$\n\nApplying ${y}^{′}(0)=5$ and noting that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =B\\sqrt{6}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n\\pi }{2}{b}_{n} \\\\ B\\sqrt{6} & =5-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n\\pi }{2}{b}_{n} \\\\ B & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{6}}-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n\\pi {b}_{n}}{2\\sqrt{6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $B$ back into ${y}_{c}=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{6}x),$ we find the missing part:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{6}x)(\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{6}}-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n\\pi {b}_{n}}{2\\sqrt{6}})+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}x)\n$$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557308 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557104 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557308 1069 "q-80911" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557308 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557308 1071 "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation $\\,{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}]x(t).$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557308 1072 #uuid "cb79f743-bef1-5e9c-b5b3-1f6ceebb3420" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557308 1074 "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\lambda \\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the matrix of our differential equation is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t/4}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t/4},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557309 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557145 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557309 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557197 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557309 1069 "q-256862" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557309 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557309 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)={e}^{x}-{x}^{3}$ in the interval $[3,4].$ Calculate the first two iterations of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point ${x}_{0}=3.6$ Round your answers to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,{x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557309 1072 #uuid "cbcfb522-f7cc-5bde-a522-5b2cd1adaa83" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557309 1074 "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({e}^{x}-{x}^{3})^{′}={e}^{x}-3{x}^{2}, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =({e}^{x}-3{x}^{2})^{′}={e}^{x}-6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-3{x}_{n}^{2}}{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-6{x}_{n}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=3.6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{0}}-3{x}_{0}^{2}}{{e}^{{x}_{0}}-6{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =3.6-\\frac{{e}^{3.6}-3(3.6)^{2}}{{e}^{3.6}-6(3.6)} \\\\  & =3.752\\,135…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{1}}-3{x}_{1}^{2}}{{e}^{{x}_{1}}-6{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =(3.752\\,135…)-\\frac{{e}^{3.752\\,135…}-3(3.752\\,135…)^{2}}{{e}^{3.752\\,135…}-6(3.752\\,135…)} \\\\  & =3.733\\,407…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}≈3.752$ and ${x}_{2}≈3.733,$ rounded to three decimal places." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557310 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557189 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557310 1069 "q-25767" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557310 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557310 1071 "Find the set of values of $x$ that satisfy $5x-1>7$ or $2\\le 2x+5<16.$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557310 1072 #uuid "cbe793da-7d16-5ef3-b7b6-788b5db16db0" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557310 1074 "We start by solving $5x-1>7:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-1 & >7 \\\\ 5x & >8 \\\\ x & >\\frac{8}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/0e/0efcf15a3abee964ad2dfc0c1dac12a0f2f43c86180d2ead869fc2ee7b8e9c76.png)\n\nThen, we solve $2\\le 2x+5<16:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & \\le 2x+5<16 \\\\ 2-5 & \\le 2x<16-5 \\\\ -3 & \\le 2x<11 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & \\le x<\\frac{11}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/22/22f51dad4248c565cd3afcf03ec9f5a8c50494ad79ed1366b3513083be27182a.png)\n\nTo graph the values of $x$ that satisfy either inequality, we overlay the two graphs on the same number line:\n\n![](images/e7/e769eea6e55f7138cbce08cb285253fc1c710c2e39384994a8b36bd43a41ea32.png)\n\nTherefore, the final solution is $x\\ge -\\frac{3}{2}.$  We can write this using interval notation as $[-\\frac{3}{2},\\infty ).$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557311 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557113 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557311 1069 "q-109257" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557311 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557311 1071 "Consider the arc of the curve $y=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}$ that lies between the lines $x=-1$ and $x=1.$ Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557311 1072 #uuid "cbe7ea72-e04a-55e6-a4f7-365b15606888" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557311 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${x}_{1}=-1$ to ${x}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,⇒\\,{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4-{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\pi x\\,{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\pi [1-(-1)] \\\\  & =8\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557312 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557172 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557312 1069 "q-11481" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557312 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557312 1071 "![](images/63/63bc2b3795556cd1f1b3b5b63c8d92816f64edcad364664db25acc0a7cc59b9e.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curve $y=3{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}$ and the $x$-axis, shown above. What integral gives the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557312 1072 #uuid "cc5e2afa-3b34-5a5a-87d2-86576b259f97" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557312 1074 "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(3-x) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=0$ and $x=3.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=3.$  \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}x(3{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}(3{x}^{3}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557313 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557117 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557313 1069 "q-147600" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557313 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557313 1071 "Suppose the following random sample was drawn from the uniform distribution $U[0,1].$ \n\n$$\n0.459,\\,0.714,\\,0.938\n$$\n\nUse these numbers to generate three random numbers that follow a probability distribution with the CDF $F(x),$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\frac{1}{1+{e}^{-x}},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nRound your answers to $3$ decimal places." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557313 1072 #uuid "d55d9f6d-31b7-5824-bc8b-1fe8f38e31d7" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557313 1074 "Let the random variable $Y∼U[0,1]$ be uniformly distributed. Suppose that the function $F(x)$ is a cumulative distribution function, where $F(a)=0,F(b)=1,$ and $F$ is strictly monotonic on $a<x<b.$ Then, the random variable $X,$ given by\n\n$$\nX={F}^{-1}(Y)\n$$\n\nis a continuous random variable with the cumulative distribution function $F(x).$\n\nSo, we need to find the inverse of $y=F(x).$ To do this, we solve for the following equation for $x,$ taking into account that $0\\le y\\le 1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{1+{e}^{-x}} \\\\ 1+{e}^{-x} & =\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ {e}^{-x} & =\\frac{1-y}{y} \\\\ -x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1-y}{y}) \\\\ x & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1-y}{y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the numbers drawn from $U[0,1]$ into the above equation:\n\n- For $y=0.459,$ we get $x=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1-0.459}{0.459})≈-0.164$\n\n- For $y=0.714,$ we get $x=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1-0.714}{0.714})≈0.915$\n\n- For $y=0.938,$ we get $x=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1-0.938}{0.938})≈2.717$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557314 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557184 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557314 1069 "q-32585" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557314 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557314 1071 "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 9\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557314 1072 #uuid "d742eff4-edcb-531d-a1ae-99b88035cdf2" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557314 1074 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-6){R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557315 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557068 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557315 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557164 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557315 1069 "q-277275" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557315 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557315 1071 "Determine the domain of $f(x)=\\sqrt{6x-5}.$\n\nThe domain is $x\\in [${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557315 1072 #uuid "ddedbc01-5d9e-5e7a-be28-fa7766a765e4" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557315 1074 "The domain of $y=\\sqrt{x}$ is\n\n$$\nx\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the domain of $f(x)=\\sqrt{6x-5},$ we replace $x$ with $6x-5$ in the above inequality. This gives\n\n$$\n6x-5\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-5 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 6x & \\ge 5 \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $y=f(x)$ is $x\\in [\\frac{5}{6},\\infty ).$\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/4d/4d0a6205a61aa0625a082d6d3bc1941ab00e0536c85844f28af873798bf6db64.png)" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557316 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557129 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557316 1069 "q-27994" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557316 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557316 1071 "Which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=(2-x)(1-x)^{2}?$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557316 1072 #uuid "de17e3d5-60e1-5541-9c18-3de6f1f6965c" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557316 1074 "The equation is already in factored form, so by the zero-product rule, we deduce that it has a single root at $x=2$ and a double root at $x=1.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(2-0)(1-0)^{2} \\\\  & =(2)(1)^{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,2),$\n\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=1,$ and\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=2.$\n\nNotice that the leading coefficient is *negative*:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(2-x)(1-x)^{2} \\\\  & =(2-x)({x}^{2}-2x+1) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph takes an $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/78/78af8854b6dd0da9eeded23cbcfa223018ca425d13d802b9e72039d906976bde.png)" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557317 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557134 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557317 1069 "q-75098" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557317 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557317 1071 "Suppose that a $3\\times 3$ matrix $H$ is reduced to row echelon form using the following elementary row operations, applied in the order in which they appear:\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $H$ has the LU factorization $H=LU,$ then $L=$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557317 1072 #uuid "e07c46c7-d38b-5617-a5f3-42fc8a91e6b5" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557317 1074 "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+1\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $H$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot H=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}H=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}H & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}H & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ H & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ H & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557318 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557087 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557318 1069 "q-28966" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557318 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557318 1071 "Find all the values of $x$ that satisfy $3x+5>11$ or $2x-3\\le 1.$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557318 1072 #uuid "e377c474-d1b6-5e34-ae2e-6bda298c1707" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557318 1074 "We start by solving $3x+5>11:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+5 & >11 \\\\ 3x & >6 \\\\ x & >2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/87/87831ea65f4caa557c8e8826faaa43a60737c3590393872902818921668787d0.png)\n\nThen, we solve $2x-3\\le 1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-3 & \\le 1 \\\\ 2x & \\le 4 \\\\ x & \\le 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/49/4965930532798f18907e5319f2fd9fe5f17d9c1969c12d9795f9867a2cb13351.png)\n\nTo graph the values of $x$ that satisfy either inequality, we overlay the two graphs on the same number line:\n\n![](images/a9/a9543d5a2add249d869a667d16bea75780b0e44e4c0d2bde2cf460733d5e9634.png)\n\nTherefore, the final solution is $x\\le 2$ or $x>2.$ We can write this using interval notation as\n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557319 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557106 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557319 1069 "q-162587" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557319 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557319 1071 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $y=-1,$ $y=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=2,$ and the surfaces $x=y-z$ and $x=1+{y}^{2}.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=i+x{z}^{3}\\,j+xz\\,k,$ the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS=\n$$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557319 1072 #uuid "e44dc84b-de4c-5508-b285-9f3d93ffdc55" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557319 1074 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{z}^{3})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xz) \\\\  & =0+0+x \\\\  & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}x\\,\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type II solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le 2,\\,y-z\\le x\\le 1+{y}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y-z}^{1+{y}^{2}}\\,x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557320 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557083 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557320 1069 "q-79026" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557320 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557320 1071 "What is the diameter of a circle if its area is $9\\pi \\,{\\text{km}}^{2}?$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557320 1072 #uuid "e818e98a-49f0-5632-a39a-42d0b7b18e71" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557320 1074 "The formula for the area of a circle is $A=\\pi {r}^{2}$, where $r$ is the radius. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ 9\\pi  & =\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ 9\\cancel{\\pi } & =\\cancel{\\pi }{r}^{2} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =9 \\\\ r & =3\\,\\text{km}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the diameter is\n\n$$\nd=2\\cdot r=2\\cdot 3=6\\,\\text{km}.\n$$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557321 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557143 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557321 1069 "q-147659" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557321 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557321 1071 "Suppose the following random sample was drawn from the uniform distribution $U[0,1].$ \n\n$$\n0.257,\\,0.498,\\,0.632\n$$\n\nUse these numbers to generate three random numbers that follow a probability distribution with the CDF $F(x),$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & \\,x<0, \\\\ {x}^{3}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 1, & \\,x>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRound your answers to $3$ decimal places." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557321 1072 #uuid "e9e69354-0b9b-54c9-b814-cf1944d62671" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557321 1074 "Let the random variable $Y∼U[0,1]$ be uniformly distributed. Suppose that the function $F(x)$ is a cumulative distribution function, where $F(a)=0,F(b)=1,$ and $F$ is strictly monotonic on $a<x<b.$ Then, the random variable $X,$ given by\n\n$$\nX={F}^{-1}(Y)\n$$\n\nis a continuous random variable with the cumulative distribution function $F(x).$\n\nSo, we need to find the inverse of $y=F(x).$ To do this, we solve the following equation for $x,$ taking into account that $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0<y<1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={x}^{3} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt[3]{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the numbers drawn from $U[0,1]$ into the above equation:\n\n- For $y=0.257,$ we get $x=\\sqrt[3]{0.257}≈0.636$\n\n- For $y=0.498,$ we get $x=\\sqrt[3]{0.498}≈0.793$\n\n- For $y=0.632,$ we get $x=\\sqrt[3]{0.632}≈0.858$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557322 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557064 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557322 1069 "q-3889" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557322 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557322 1071 "![](images/40/406bfedb1113d8e953fcac9739a144027a592ac5d7e2409604147a1b7f258a02.png)\n\nThe graph above is a translation of $y=\\sqrt{x}.$ Identify the equation that generated the graph." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557322 1072 #uuid "eb053305-3f48-5a05-bfa6-336398fa92e3" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557322 1074 "To obtain the given graph, we can perform the following transformations:\n\n- Take the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\n- Translate the graph to the left by $1$ unit to get the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x+1}.$\n\n- Translate the result down by $4$ units to get the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x+1}-4.$\n\nThe result is the solid blue curve shown below.\n\n![](images/7e/7ef3c8f48902dda0c80fb5fbd3db74dee4cd3ea71c2711878911f6bb0090d730.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y=\\sqrt{x+1}-4.$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557323 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557158 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557323 1069 "q-104015" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557323 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557323 1071 "Find the set of values of $x$ that satisfy $-8<4x-4<8$ or $2-x>3.$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557323 1072 #uuid "f2b14810-a4bb-5d69-856a-68799a4e514a" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557323 1074 "We start by solving $-8<4x-4<8:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8 & <4x-4<8 \\\\ -4 & <4x<12 \\\\ -1 & <x<3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/fd/fdf77a12d535974088d36ca7508443a2aab00bb147fde65a17fc06cf5f0bce23.png)\n\nThen, we solve $2-x>3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-x & >3 \\\\ -x & >1 \\\\ \\frac{-x}{-1} & <\\frac{1}{-1} \\\\ x & <-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/8a/8a1af65f2cebbcaeb73c138be5d1cd67d714aacb8db1c995f29b930c06b953fe.png)\n\nTo graph the values of $x$ that satisfy either inequality, we overlay the two graphs on the same number line:\n\n![](images/be/be8cace3271cdfa133d1b647359400681db04f504560ad306e3e1807eb1502d9.png)\n\nTherefore, the final solution is $-1<x<3$ or $x<-1.$ We can write this using interval notation as $(-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (-1,3).$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557324 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557066 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557324 1069 "q-96069" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557324 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557324 1071 "What type of quadratic curve is defined by the following equation?\n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}-4{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=13\n$$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557324 1072 #uuid "f46d525e-1765-5c37-bb92-276f989431a5" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557324 1074 "First, we complete the squares for the variables ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}-4{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2} & =13 \\\\ 2({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1})-3({x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{2}) & =13 \\\\ 2({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}+1-1)-3({x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{2}+1-1) & =13 \\\\ 2({x}_{1}-1)^{2}-2-3({x}_{2}-1)^{2}+3 & =13 \\\\ 2({x}_{1}-1)^{2}-3({x}_{2}-1)^{2} & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}={x}_{1}-1 \\\\ {y}_{2}={x}_{2}-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1}^{2}-3{y}_{2}^{2} & =12 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{6}-\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{6})^{2}}-\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis represents a hyperbola with semiaxes $\\sqrt{6}$ and $2.$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557325 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557077 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557325 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557162 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557325 1069 "q-334856" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557325 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557325 1071 "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,\\text{d}s$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557325 1072 #uuid "f491a1e5-e94d-5b1b-9331-94aa4176665c" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557325 1074 "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)={{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+{{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{⏟}}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At}\\,x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]+{\\int }_{0}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557326 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557132 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557326 1069 "q-147602" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557326 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557326 1071 "Suppose the following random sample was drawn from the uniform distribution $U[0,1].$ \n\n$$\n0.256,\\,0.783,\\,0.809\n$$\n\nUse these numbers to generate three random numbers that follow a probability distribution with the CDF $F(x),$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & \\,x<-1, \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}(x+1), & \\,-1\\le x\\le 3, \\\\ 1, & \\,x>3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557326 1072 #uuid "f583a782-477b-5b0e-85b4-2269efa11788" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557326 1074 "Let the random variable $Y∼U[0,1]$ be uniformly distributed. Suppose that the function $F(x)$ is a cumulative distribution function, where $F(a)=0,F(b)=1,$ and $F$ is strictly monotonic on $a<x<b.$ Then, the random variable $X,$ given by\n\n$$\nX={F}^{-1}(Y)\n$$\n\nis a continuous random variable with the cumulative distribution function $F(x).$\n\nSo, we need to find the inverse of $y=F(x).$ To do this, we solve the following equation for $x,$ taking into account that $-1\\le x\\le 3$ and $0<y<1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+1) \\\\ 4y & =x+1 \\\\ x & =4y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the numbers drawn from $U[0,1]$ into the above equation:\n\n- For $y=0.256,$ we get $x=4(0.256)-1=0.024$\n\n- For $y=0.783,$ we get $x=4(0.783)-1=2.132$\n\n- For $y=0.809,$ we get $x=4(0.809)-1=2.236$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557327 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557128 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557327 1069 "q-163156" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557327 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557327 1071 "Consider the function $f(x,y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=6xy,\\,D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 4x\\}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ Find $a$ given that $b=2.$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557327 1072 #uuid "f6236dff-5836-5e2b-be74-a41334b247e2" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557327 1074 "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}(6xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4x}6xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[3x{y}^{2}]_{y=0}^{y=4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}48{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[12{x}^{4}]_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4x}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[y]_{y=0}^{y=4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[2{x}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $b=2.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =f(a,2)\\cdot 2 \\\\ 12 & =(6a\\cdot 2)\\cdot 2 \\\\ 12 & =24a \\\\ a & =\\frac{12}{24} \\\\ a & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $(\\frac{1}{2},2)\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $a=\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557328 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557208 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557328 1069 "q-112137" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557328 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557328 1071 "Given that $(x-1)$ is a factor of $f(x)={x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+9x-4,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557328 1072 #uuid "f6ba8710-6e4b-5d1b-8f82-9bf7f2de8b0d" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557328 1074 "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x-1)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x-1)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ 1 & 1 & -6 & 9 & -4 \\\\  &  & 1 & -5 & 4 \\\\  & 1 & -5 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{f(x)}{x-1}={x}^{2}-5x+4\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-1)({x}^{2}-5x+4).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-1)(x-1)(x-4)=(x-4)(x-1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have the cubic in factored form, we can tell that it has a single root at $x=4$ and a double root at $x=1.$\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(0-4)(0-1)^{2} \\\\  & =(-4)(-1)^{2} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,-4),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=4,$ and\n\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=1.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/62/62b826632a520d1c1d2180b62e528b2a9d5264d6102419822a5194e90196d9f2.png)" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557329 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557063 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557329 1069 "q-82670" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557329 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557329 1071 "Which of the following tables describes a function that has $0$ as a root?" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557329 1072 #uuid "f7198a9b-72ee-54fc-b7ac-cda83b608de8" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557329 1074 "The number $0$ is a root of $f(x)$ if $f(0)=0.$ Of the given tables, only the following table has this property:\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $4$ | $2$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $-5$ | $-7$ |" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557330 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557138 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557330 1069 "q-96091" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557330 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557330 1071 "What type of quadratic curve is defined by the following equation?\n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{2}\n$$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557330 1072 #uuid "f72287c6-b1eb-590a-839e-db294cf79f90" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557330 1074 "First, we complete the squares for the variable ${x}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{1} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 2({x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{1})+2{x}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 2({x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{1}+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2})+2{x}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 2{({x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-2{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 2{({x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 2{({x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2} & =2 \\\\ {({x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}={x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ {y}_{2}={x}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis represents a circle of radius $1.$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557331 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557074 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557331 1069 "q-343762" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557331 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557331 1071 "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-2y=f(x),\\,y(1)=-4\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is a $2$-periodic function. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(1)=0.$*\n\nThe solution is \n\n$y(x)∼$   \n{{field-1}} \n$+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557331 1072 #uuid "fa1dbe09-82df-56ed-9d56-cd22c9d94295" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557331 1074 "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{c}$ is the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we find the homogeneous solution. The general solution of ${y}^{′}-2y=0$ is \n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNext, we identify the form of the general solution. Because the operator ${y}^{′}-2y$ involves a first derivative, a periodic forcing function $f(x)$ results in a particular solution containing both sine and cosine terms:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼A{e}^{2x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the initial condition $y(1)=-4.$ Using the hint that the particular solution part evaluates to zero at $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =A{e}^{2(1)}+{y}_{p}(1) \\\\ -4 & =A{e}^{2}+0 \\\\ A & =-4{e}^{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $A$ back into the homogeneous part:\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=(-4{e}^{-2}){e}^{2x}=-4{e}^{2x-2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼-4{e}^{2x-2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)).\n$$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557332 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557090 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557332 1069 "q-71430" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557332 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557332 1071 "Suzie takes $\\frac{2}{3}$ hours to read $22$ pages of a certain book. How many pages can she read in $1$ hour?" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557332 1072 #uuid "fab8d7a2-8042-5e4e-8fd9-ae407a4d21d4" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557332 1074 "To find the number of pages Suzie can read in $1$ hour, we compute the unit rate of pages per hour. We do this by dividing the number of pages Suzie reads, $22,$ by the number of hours, $\\frac{2}{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n22÷\\frac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by a whole number is equivalent to multiplying the fraction by the reciprocal of that whole number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}22÷\\frac{2}{3} & =22\\times \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{22}{1}\\times \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{22\\times 3}{1\\times 2} \\\\  & =\\frac{66}{2} \\\\  & =33\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Suzie can read $33$ pages in $1$ hour." 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557333 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557187 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557333 1069 "q-112166" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557333 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557333 1071 "Given that $x+2$ is a factor of $f(x)=-{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+8x+12,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n*(Note: Figures not drawn to scale)*" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557333 1072 #uuid "fb4e0d8f-3854-5a60-be7f-5cac9da0624a" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557333 1074 "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x+2)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -2 & -1 & -1 & 8 & 12 \\\\  &  & 2 & -2 & -12 \\\\  & -1 & 1 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{f(x)}{x+2} & =-{x}^{2}+x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+2)(-{x}^{2}+x+6) \\\\  & =-(x+2)({x}^{2}-x-6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\nf(x)=-(x+2)(x+2)(x-3)=-(x-3)(x+2)^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have the cubic in factored form, we can tell that it has a single root at $x=3$ and a double root at $x=-2.$\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-(0-3)(0+2)^{2} \\\\  & =-(-3)(2)^{2} \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,12),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=3,$ and\n\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=-2.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph takes an $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/99/99063a71f1a368c2f27bd9b4d3817ea8f39e34789d26074f70c68333667cd90f.png)" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557334 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557107 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557334 1069 "q-75155" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557334 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557334 1071 "Which of the following curves is generated by $y=\\frac{1}{3}(2-x)(3+x)^{2}?$" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557334 1072 #uuid "ff659825-07ff-583c-bc20-8ee310e82141" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557334 1074 "The equation is already in factored form, so by the zero-product rule, we deduce that it has a single root at $x=2$ and a double root at $x=-3.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{3}(2-0)(3+0)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2)(3)^{2}}{3} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,6),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=2,$ and\n\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=-3.$\n\nNotice that the leading coefficient is *negative*:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{3}(2-x)(3+x)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2-x)({x}^{2}+6x+9) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph takes an $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/dc/dc748825dca638e83ad9e09d7144365abbb3024ccb6474673c0557e3e1c21325.png)" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557335 1058 #edb/ref 17592186557373 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557335 1062 #uuid "0398fe30-8e44-5ba6-8fd1-a43e6091f841" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557335 1063 #edb/ref 17592186167246 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557336 1062 #uuid "03e32401-df3c-5835-ab23-3c8cb7d34eca" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557336 1063 #edb/ref 17592186557284 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557337 1058 #edb/ref 17592186557339 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557337 1062 #uuid "098818fd-a8d8-5d01-87be-46e181cc7f9d" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557337 1063 #edb/ref 17592186557325 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557338 1058 #edb/ref 17592186557407 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557338 1062 #uuid "0bd04562-8813-5c77-9599-36a65de31f18" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557338 1063 #edb/ref 17592186557273 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557339 1058 #edb/ref 17592186557401 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557339 1062 #uuid "0df3f949-5bbe-5136-b7e7-4edc6e213b6f" 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557339 1063 #edb/ref 17592186229675 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557340 1058 #edb/ref 17592186557404 13194139536011 17592186045417 true] [17592186557340 1062 #uuid "0f3e5941-5ae6-550c-8cd9-e1b74